"""Authored topic depth and distinct worked examples for networks and aptitude."""
from . import P, E

P('9-layers-switching-layers', 'Principles of Layering', r'''
Think of a layer as offering a service while carrying its own addressing and control information. Encapsulation changes the unit whose length is being measured: application bytes become transport payload, then network payload, then link payload. Work outward through the headers rather than counting every header repeatedly. At a forwarding boundary, ask which unit the device terminates and reconstructs. A plain Ethernet switch forwards a frame, whereas an IP router removes one link envelope and builds another. These exercises exclude tunnels, NAT, optional headers and physical coding unless explicitly supplied; those mechanisms can change the otherwise simple address and size trace.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A TCP application sends 1000 bytes. TCP and IPv4 headers are 20 bytes each; Ethernet adds a 14-byte header and 4-byte FCS. Exclude padding, preamble and interframe gap. Find each encapsulated length.', 'Transport unit::1000+20=1020\\ bytes::TCP encloses the application payload once.~Network unit::1020+20=1040\\ bytes::The IP payload includes the complete TCP segment.~Link unit::1040+14+4=1058\\ bytes::Ethernet encloses the IP packet and appends its check field.', 'TCP segment 1020 bytes; IP packet 1040; Ethernet frame 1058.', 'Added overhead is 58 bytes, and 1000+58 independently reproduces the final length.'),
    E('application', 'An IPv4 path MTU is 1500 bytes. TCP has a 32-byte header and IPv4 a 20-byte header. The sender avoids IP fragmentation. What maximum application payload fits in one segment, and does an Ethernet header reduce this IP MTU budget?', 'Identify the budget::::The 1500-byte limit applies to the complete IP packet, not its Ethernet envelope.~Remove network overhead::1500-20=1480\\ bytes::This is the maximum TCP segment length.~Remove transport overhead::1480-32=1448\\ bytes::The remaining bytes can hold application data; Ethernet overhead is outside the stated IP budget.', 'Maximum application payload 1448 bytes; Ethernet does not further reduce the stated IP MTU budget.', '1448+32+20=1500, while adding link overhead produces a larger frame rather than an oversized IP packet.'),
    E('trap', 'A plain learning Ethernet switch forwards a frame between two ports on one LAN. No router, NAT or tunnel is involved. Must it replace the IP destination with the next port’s address?', 'Locate the forwarding layer::::The switch selects a port using a link-layer destination address.~Identify the IP role::::The enclosed IP destination names the network-layer endpoint; a port number is not a replacement IP address.~Apply the assumptions::::Ordinary frame switching does not rewrite the IP destination merely because the outgoing physical port differs.', 'No; changing switch ports does not require changing the packet’s IP destination.', 'The destination host can receive the same addressed IP packet after several LAN switches, demonstrating separation of link forwarding from network addressing.'))

P('9-layers-switching-circuit-session', 'Circuit switching', r'''
Reservation and useful transmission are separate accounting problems. A circuit can hold a share of every traversed link even while its application is silent. Admission therefore depends on the smallest available capacity along the complete path, not on one link's free rate or recent average traffic. Once admitted, transfer time uses the reserved end-to-end rate plus the explicitly serial setup and propagation components. When comparing a circuit with another service, write both complete delay expressions and solve their inequality; setup can make small transfers slower despite a better sustained rate. Decimal Mbps denotes millions of bits per second in these exercises.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A circuit route traverses links with free capacities 12, 9 and 15 Mbps. Every admitted circuit reserves 3 Mbps on each link. How many additional identical circuits fit?', 'Count per-link slots::(12/3,9/3,15/3)=(4,3,5)::A circuit must obtain capacity at every hop.~Choose the bottleneck::\\min(4,3,5)=3::The middle link runs out first.~Test the next request::4(3)=12>9\\ Mbps::A fourth reservation fails even though the other links have room.', 'Three additional circuits fit.', 'After three admissions the middle link has zero spare capacity, while the other links retain 3 and 6 Mbps.'),
    E('application', 'For L data bits, circuit A takes 50 ms setup plus L/4 Mbps transfer. Service B takes 20 ms fixed delay plus L/2 Mbps transfer. Ignore other costs. Find the size where A becomes no slower.', 'Write elapsed times::T_A=0.05+L/(4\\times10^6),\\quad T_B=0.02+L/(2\\times10^6)::Both include their supplied startup costs.~Solve the comparison::0.03\\le L/(4\\times10^6)::Move the faster circuit’s transfer term to the other side.~Find the threshold::L\\ge120000\\ bits::At this size the saved serialization time offsets the extra setup.', 'A is no slower for L≥120, 000 bits.', 'At the threshold both times are 80 ms: A is 50+30 and B is 20+60 ms.'),
    E('trap', 'A 20 Mbps link has five active circuit reservations of 4 Mbps each. Four applications are momentarily silent. Can a sixth 4 Mbps circuit be admitted without changing existing reservations?', 'Count reserved capacity::5(4)=20\\ Mbps::Admission uses commitments, including silent sessions.~Distinguish utilization::::Current useful traffic can be only 4 Mbps while reserved capacity remains 20 Mbps.~Test admission::20+4=24>20\\ Mbps::The sixth commitment exceeds link capacity.', 'No under the reservation model; silence does not release a circuit.', 'If the four idle circuits were explicitly torn down, the admission result would change; merely observing no data is insufficient.'))

P('9-layers-switching-packet-pipeline', 'Packet switching', r'''
Packet completion is governed by service order and available interfaces. On a single nonpreemptive link, queued packets serialize one after another. Across independent store-and-forward links, different packets can occupy different links simultaneously, but a later link cannot start a packet before its full arrival or before finishing earlier queued work. Use start=max(arrival, previous completion), then add that link's serialization duration. Packetization can reduce multi-hop completion through overlap while increasing transmitted bytes through repeated headers. It does not automatically improve a one-link transfer with no overlap opportunity. These models use FIFO queues, no propagation and no competing traffic unless stated otherwise.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Three 2000-bit packets arrive together at a FIFO1 Mbps link at t=0. No other delays occur. Find their completion and queue-wait times.', 'Service duration::2000/10^6=2\\ ms::Each packet occupies the link for two milliseconds.~Schedule FIFO::::Packet transmissions are 0–2, 2–4 and 4–6 ms because the link serves one at a time.~Separate wait and completion::::Waiting before service is 0, 2, 4 ms; complete departure is 2, 4, 6 ms.', 'Completion times 2, 4, 6 ms; queue waits 0, 2, 4 ms.', 'Three service intervals total 6 ms, and each completion equals its queue wait plus 2 ms.'),
    E('application', 'Three equal packets take 1 ms on link A and 2 ms on link B. A store-and-forward router connects the independent FIFO links; all packets are ready at t=0. Ignore propagation. Find the last departure.', 'Arrive at the router::::Link A finishes the packets at 1, 2 and 3 ms.~Schedule the slower link::::B serves packet 1 during 1–3 ms; packet 2 arrives at 2 but waits, then runs 3–5 ms.~Finish the pipeline::::Packet 3 arrived at 3 and follows packet 2 during 5–7 ms.', 'The final packet leaves at 7 ms.', 'B sends three packets for 6 ms starting at 1 ms, so 1+6=7; multiplying three end-to-end 3 ms delays would incorrectly give 9.'),
    E('trap', 'Send 1000 payload bytes over one 8 Mbps link. A single packet has a 20-byte header; splitting into two 500-byte packets adds 20 bytes to each. Ignore all other costs. Does splitting reduce completion time?', 'Count the unsplit wire data::1000+20=1020\\ bytes::Only one header is paid.~Count split wire data::2(500+20)=1040\\ bytes::The payload stays the same but a second header is transmitted.~Convert link time::T_1=1020\\ \\mu s,\\quad T_2=1040\\ \\mu s::At 8 Mbps each byte takes one microsecond and there is no second-link pipeline.', 'Splitting is 20 μs slower here.', 'The extra 20-byte header explains exactly the time difference; multi-hop overlap was explicitly absent.'))

P('9-layers-switching-switching', 'Virtual circuit', r'''
A virtual circuit combines packet transfer with installed connection state. A forwarding entry associates an incoming interface and local label with an outgoing interface and possibly a different label. The same numeric label can be reused on different links when lookup includes the interface. Trace that tuple at every hop rather than assuming one globally meaningful identifier. Setup determines forwarding context, but bandwidth reservation, reliability and recovery are independent supplied guarantees. A broken link can invalidate the established path even if the end host's destination address remains unchanged. These examples use locally significant labels and explicit setup state, without implying dedicated physical capacity.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Router R maps incoming(port 1, label 7) to outgoing(port 3, label 12). The next router S maps incoming(port 2, label 12) to outgoing(port 4, label 5). Trace one packet.', 'Lookup at R::::The incoming interface and label identify R’s established circuit entry.~Rewrite for the next link::::R forwards on port 3 with local label 12, rather than retaining 7 everywhere.~Lookup at S::::S uses its incoming port 2 and label 12 entry, then sends on port 4 with label 5.', 'Labels along the trace are 7→12→5; the forwarding context determines the path.', 'Every transmitted label equals the receiving router’s stated lookup label; no globally constant label was required.'),
    E('application', 'A router has entries(port 1, label 4)→port 3 and(port 2, label 4)→port 5. Two established circuits arrive with label 4 on the two incoming ports. Is the table ambiguous, and how many lookup contexts exist?', 'Identify the complete key::::The model uses incoming interface together with the label.~Compare both packets::::Their numeric labels match but their input interfaces differ, giving two distinct keys.~Forward independently::::The port 1 packet uses port 3; the port 2 packet uses port 5.', 'The table is unambiguous and contains two distinct lookup contexts.', 'If lookup ignored the interface, the two destinations would conflict; including the stipulated interface resolves the apparent collision.'),
    E('trap', 'A virtual-circuit service installs a fixed path but explicitly offers shared queues and best-effort delivery. A student concludes that packets cannot be dropped because the connection exists. Is that valid?', 'List the stated guarantee::::Installed path state tells routers how to forward matching packets.~Examine queue behavior::::Shared finite queues can overflow under contention; path state does not create unlimited buffering.~Evaluate the conclusion::::No loss-free or reservation contract was supplied, so connection orientation alone cannot imply reliable delivery.', 'The conclusion is invalid; an established best-effort virtual circuit can lose packets.', 'A packet arriving at a full queue provides a counterexample consistent with every supplied service guarantee.'))

P('9-layers-switching-delays', 'Performance metrics', r'''
Latency measures one item's elapsed journey, while throughput measures sustained delivered data per time. They respond differently to link speed, distance and protocol waiting. Serialization depends on bits divided by bit rate; propagation depends on distance divided by signal speed. A large bandwidth-delay product means a link can carry many bits simultaneously without making each bit propagate faster. For stop-and-wait, the sender's idle acknowledgment wait can dominate utilization even on a fast link. Keep complete cycle times distinct from extra penalties and specify whether the given delay is one-way or round trip before calculating. Decimal SI units are used below.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A 1000-bit packet crosses one 50 Mbps link of length 100 km with signal speed 2×10⁸ m/s. No queue or processing delays exist. Find last-bit arrival time after transmission begins.', 'Serialize::1000/(50\\times10^6)=20\\ \\mu s::This is the time to launch the last bit.~Propagate::100000/(2\\times10^8)=500\\ \\mu s::Every bit then travels the link distance.~Add the serial stages::20+500=520\\ \\mu s::Last-bit arrival includes its launch delay and propagation.', 'Last bit arrives after 520 μs.', 'The first bit arrives after 500 μs, and the last follows 20 μs later, independently reproducing 520.'),
    E('application', 'Stop-and-wait sends 8000 data bits over 8 Mbps. One-way propagation is 4 ms; ACK serialization and processing are negligible. There is no loss. Find utilization and useful throughput.', 'Data transmission::T_d=8000/(8\\times10^6)=1\\ ms::One millisecond launches the packet.~Complete the feedback cycle::T_{cycle}=1+4+4=9\\ ms::The ACK returns after the forward and reverse propagation legs.~Compute delivery rate::U=1/9,\\quad throughput=8000/0.009\\approx888889\\ bit/s::Only the data-launch portion keeps the sender transmitting.', 'Utilization 1/9≈11.11%; useful throughput≈0.889 Mbps.', 'Multiplying link rate 8 Mbps by 1/9 gives the same throughput; omitting the return leg would exaggerate utilization.'),
    E('trap', 'A 100 Mbps link has one-way propagation 10 ms. Another link doubles its bit rate but has the same distance and signal speed. Does this halve propagation, and how does one-way bandwidth-delay product change?', 'Locate the propagation inputs::::Distance and signal speed are unchanged, so propagation remains 10 ms.~Compute initial in-flight capacity::10^8(0.01)=10^6\\ bits::Bandwidth-delay product counts bits launched during the one-way delay.~Increase rate::2\\times10^8(0.01)=2\\times10^6\\ bits::The pipeline can contain twice as many bits without any bit traveling faster.', 'Propagation stays 10 ms; one-way bandwidth-delay product doubles from 1 to 2 Mbit.', 'Rate affects serialization and pipeline occupancy, whereas the supplied propagation formula contains no link bit rate.'))

P('9-link-layer-bit-stuffing', 'Data link layer', r'''
Framing protects boundaries so a receiver can separate a continuous physical stream into units. Delimiter protection must preserve all original payload bits, including zero bits that resemble inserted escape information. A sender tracks consecutive ones and inserts an extra zero according to the stated rule; a receiver applies the exact inverse inside identified frame boundaries. Count payload, stuffed interior and delimiter lengths separately. The data-link layer can also implement acknowledgment protocols, but framing alone does not retransmit a lost frame or verify payload integrity. The exercises below use the five-one stuffing convention and distinct external flags, excluding physical coding and CRC fields.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Stuff payload 11111011111 by inserting 0 after every five consecutive original ones. Find the transmitted interior.', 'Process the first run::::The first five ones require a stuffed zero, giving 111110.~Preserve the original zero::::The next payload bit is also 0; it must remain, yielding 1111100.~Process the final run::::Its five ones require another stuffed 0, completing 1111100111110.', 'Interior 1111100111110, containing 13 bits for 11 payload bits.', 'Destuffing removes exactly two inserted zeros and retains the original middle zero, recovering 11111011111.'),
    E('application', 'A frame uses two 8-bit flags and a payload of 100 consecutive ones. Stuff a zero after each group of five ones, including the final group. No other fields exist. Find wire length and payload fraction.', 'Count inserted bits::100/5=20::Every five-one group triggers an insertion.~Add all framing fields::L=100+20+2(8)=136\\ bits::Flags are outside the stuffed interior.~Compute useful fraction::100/136=25/34\\approx73.53\\%::Payload bits are the numerator; all transmitted bits are the denominator.', 'Wire length 136 bits; payload fraction 25/34≈73.53%.', 'The interior has 120 bits and flags contribute 16, so a second independent length sum gives 136.'),
    E('trap', 'A sender stuffs five-one runs, but a receiver removes every zero after any four ones. Test original payload 11110 and explain whether this is a valid inverse.', 'Apply the sender rule::::Four ones do not trigger stuffing; transmitted interior remains 11110.~Apply the proposed receiver::::It removes the original trailing 0 after four ones, producing 1111.~Compare data::::The recovered payload is shorter and differs from the original, so the receiver destroyed real data.', 'The proposed receiver is invalid; sender and receiver must use the same five-one rule.', 'A genuine inverse would leave 11110 unchanged because no stuffed bit was inserted; this counterexample uses no transmission error.'))

P('9-link-layer-framing', 'Error detection', r'''
An error detector adds constraints to a codeword so some altered patterns fall outside the legal set. Detection succeeds when the receiver's check distinguishes the corrupted word, not merely because redundant bits exist. Simple parity depends on the number of flipped bits modulo two; it cannot determine the flipped location or catch every multi-bit change. For a linear code, an error pattern that is itself an allowed zero-syndrome pattern can transform one legal codeword into another. Use exact bit strings to demonstrate an undetected error and distinguish detection capability from correction capability. These parity exercises use an appended even-parity bit and no additional checks.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Append even parity to 10100, then inspect received 101001 after one parity-bit flip.', 'Count the data ones::::10100 contains two ones, so its correct appended parity bit is 0.~Form the legal word::::The transmitted codeword is 101000 with even total parity.~Check reception::::101001 contains three ones, so its parity check fails and detects corruption.', 'Transmit 101000; received 101001 is detected as erroneous.', 'The supplied corruption changes one bit, which toggles the parity of the total protected word.'),
    E('application', 'Use separate even parity for each 4-bit row: data rows 1010 and 0111. Find both parity bits. Then one data bit in each row flips; do both row checks detect an error?', 'Encode both rows::::1010 has two ones and needs 0; 0111 has three ones and needs 1.~Track each row independently::::Each row experiences one flip, so each row’s total parity toggles.~Apply both checks::::Both protected rows now have odd parity even though the combined frame suffered two flips.', 'Row parity bits 0 and 1; both row checks detect their respective single flip.', 'Detection depends on the protected group: two total flips do not cancel when they occur in different independently checked rows.'),
    E('trap', 'Even-parity word 1100 is transmitted. Its first two bits flip, producing 0000. Can parity detect the corruption or locate it?', 'Check the original::::1100 has two ones and is a legal even-parity word.~Check the corrupted word::::0000 has zero ones, also an even count.~Interpret the result::::The check passes despite two changed bits, and a passing parity check supplies no position information.', 'This two-bit corruption is undetected; parity cannot locate or correct it.', 'Both words satisfy the same parity constraint while representing different protected data, an explicit undetected-error counterexample.'))

P('9-link-layer-collision-timing', 'Medium Access Control', r'''
Access protocols determine who may transmit and how contenders react to contention. Carrier sensing reduces some collisions but cannot eliminate simultaneous starts separated by propagation delay. Collision detection requires overlap between a sender's transmission and the returning collision indication; randomized backoff instead governs the next attempt after a collision. In slotted ALOHA, slot alignment limits the vulnerable interval but successful delivery still requires exactly one transmitter in a slot. Always identify which access protocol the calculation assumes. The examples distinguish deterministic propagation timing, probabilistic access success and post-collision scheduling; their formulas should not be exchanged merely because all involve a shared medium.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Two stations on a shared bus are 10 μs apart. A starts at t=0; B starts at t=9 μs before hearing A. Ignoring other delays, when does A first receive B’s interfering signal?', 'Confirm a legal simultaneous start::::A’s signal reaches B only at 10 μs, so B can still sense idle at 9 μs.~Propagate B’s signal back::9+10=19\\ \\mu s::Its interfering transmission needs the same one-way travel time to A.~Compare sender timing::::A must still be transmitting at 19 μs to observe this particular collision while sending.', 'A first receives B’s signal at 19 μs.', 'The start-to-start difference is 9 μs and return propagation is 10 μs; A has not heard B at its own 10 μs mark.'),
    E('application', 'In slotted ALOHA three independent stations each transmit with probability 1/3 in a slot. Find the probability that exactly one packet succeeds in that slot.', 'Choose the successful sender::::There are three disjoint choices for the only transmitter.~Compute one choice::(1/3)(2/3)^2=4/27::That sender transmits and both others remain silent.~Combine disjoint events::P(success)=3(4/27)=4/9::Multiple transmitters collide and zero transmitters leave the slot idle.', 'Success probability 4/9.', 'Idle probability 8/27 and multiple-transmitter probability 7/27 give 8/27+12/27+7/27=1.'),
    E('trap', 'After a collision, two stations use independently uniform backoff choices from{0, 1, 2, 3}. A student says distinct stations necessarily choose distinct delays. Find the probability of choosing the same delay.', 'Count all paired choices::4\\times4=16::Independent uniform selections create sixteen equally likely ordered pairs.~List equal choices::::The matching pairs are(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 3).~Compute recurrence risk::4/16=1/4::Random backoff can still choose an equal delay; it reduces risk rather than guaranteeing uniqueness.', 'Same-delay probability 1/4; the claim is false.', 'Summing four events each of probability 1/16 gives 1/4 independently.'))

P('9-link-layer-ethernet', 'Ethernet', r'''
A learning Ethernet switch builds forwarding knowledge from source addresses, not from destinations it has merely seen named. Each table entry records the input port where that source was observed. A known unicast destination is sent toward its recorded port unless that port is the ingress port; an unknown unicast is flooded across eligible other ports. Aging can remove entries, and a moved host can update them. This describes one loop-free VLAN with ordinary bridge learning; spanning-tree control, security filtering and multicast policies are excluded. Keep switched full-duplex collision behavior distinct from the older shared half-duplex medium while tracing the same frame addresses.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A three-port learning switch starts with an empty table. Frame source A, destination B arrives on port 1. Trace learning and forwarding within one VLAN.', 'Learn the source::::Record A→port 1 because the incoming frame reveals A’s location.~Look up the destination::::No B entry exists; observing B as a destination does not teach its port.~Flood eligible outputs::::Send copies on ports 2 and 3, excluding the ingress port 1.', 'Table gains A→1; the unknown unicast is flooded to ports 2 and 3.', 'Only the source’s actual arrival provides location evidence, so the table still has no B entry.'),
    E('application', 'A switch table contains A→1, B→2, C→3. A frame from B to A arrives on port 3 after B moves. Determine the updated table and output port.', 'Update current source evidence::::Replace B’s stale port 2 entry with B→3.~Find the target::::A still maps to port 1.~Forward known unicast::::Send on port 1 only; port 2 is neither the source location nor the target.', 'Update B→3 and forward only on port 1.', 'A reply from A to B would now use port 3, confirming that source learning follows the move.'),
    E('trap', 'A known destination A maps to port 1. A frame from another host behind that same port arrives on port 1 addressed to A. Should the switch flood it to all other ports?', 'Read the destination entry::::The known target is reachable through the ingress port.~Identify the local path::::Both hosts are behind that port in this bridge model; sending elsewhere cannot help delivery.~Filter the frame::::Ordinary forwarding does not send it back through the ingress or flood unrelated ports.', 'Filter it rather than flooding; no other output port is needed.', 'Unknown-destination flooding does not apply because A already has a valid table entry.'))

P('9-link-layer-crc', 'CRC', r'''
CRC arithmetic is polynomial arithmetic over two coefficients: addition and subtraction are XOR, with no decimal borrow. Each leading one determines where to align the generator before canceling that term. Keep the generator degree separate from its bit-string length, and retain leading zeros when recording the check field. Reception divides the complete protected word; zero remainder means the word satisfies the code constraint, not proof that no bits changed. An error polynomial divisible by the generator has zero syndrome and can escape detection. These small examples use a nonzero constant generator coefficient and direct augmented-data construction, excluding implementation-specific initial registers or final XOR conventions.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Data bits are 1101 and generator 101 (degree 2). Construct the systematic CRC codeword using two appended zeros.', 'Augment the message::::1101 becomes 110100 before polynomial division.~Divide with aligned XOR::::110100 XOR101000 gives 011100; XOR010100 gives 001000; XOR001010 gives 000010, so remainder is 10.~Attach the check bits::::Replace the two appended zeros with 10, yielding 110110.', 'CRC bits 10; codeword 110110.', 'Dividing 110110 by 101 produces zero remainder: the final residual 001010 cancels exactly.'),
    E('application', 'With generator 1011 (degree 3), determine whether a burst error polynomial 10100 is detected, without needing the original valid codeword.', 'Use linearity::::A valid word has zero remainder, so reception’s syndrome is the error polynomial’s remainder.~Align the generator::::10100 XOR10110 leaves 00010, giving nonzero remainder 010.~Interpret the check::::The altered word fails divisibility and the error is detected.', 'Detected; the error remainder is 010.', 'Polynomial division gives 10100=(10)(1011)+010 over GF(2), independently reproducing the nonzero syndrome.'),
    E('trap', 'Generator 101 defines a degree 2 CRC. The error pattern is 1010, exactly the generator shifted left once. Is this nonzero error always detected?', 'Factor the error::e(x)=xg(x)::Its polynomial is an exact multiple of the generator.~Compute the syndrome::::1010 divided by 101 has remainder 00.~Apply the receiver rule::::A zero syndrome passes the CRC even though two protected bits have flipped.', 'This error is undetected.', 'XOR of any valid codeword with a multiple of g remains divisible by g; redundancy does not guarantee detection of every pattern.'))

P('9-routing-dv', 'Distance vector routing', r'''
A distance-vector advertisement is a neighbor's estimated suffix cost, so the receiving router adds its own outgoing link before comparing candidates. Maintain the chosen next hop alongside the number; advertisements alone do not contain the complete topology or prove that suffixes are independent. A link-cost change can alter the best route even while neighboring advertisements remain unchanged. When several destinations are advertised, calculate each column separately rather than selecting one globally best neighbor. The exercises assume additive nonnegative costs, explicitly available routes and stated update snapshots. Unreachable values are treated as infinity rather than as zero or an attractive missing cost.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Router R can reach A with cost 3 and B with cost 1. A advertises D=2, E=7; B advertises D=6, E=4. Find R’s routes for both destinations.', 'Calculate D candidates::3+2=5,\\quad1+6=7::Include each local hop cost before comparing.~Calculate E candidates::3+7=10,\\quad1+4=5::The same neighbor need not win every destination.~Install next hops::::D uses A at cost 5; E uses B at cost 5.', 'D→A with cost 5; E→B with cost 5.', 'Both selected totals equal actual supplied local-plus-suffix sums; choosing B solely for its cheaper first hop would fail for D.'),
    E('application', 'R initially has links to A cost 2 and B cost 5. Both advertise destination D costs 4 and 1 respectively. Break ties in favor of A. R–A then increases to cost 4 while advertisements stay unchanged. Trace the route change.', 'Initial candidates::2+4=6,\\quad5+1=6::The tie rule initially selects A.~Update the changed edge::4+4=8::Only the candidate through A changes under the supplied snapshot.~Compare again::6<8::R now selects B with total 6.', 'Initial route via A at 6; after the change via B at 6.', 'The installed distance can remain numerically unchanged while its next hop changes; recording only the cost misses the update.'),
    E('trap', 'A neighbor’s vector omits destination X and the protocol defines omission as unreachable. Its link cost is 1. Another neighbor has link cost 4 and advertises X=3. May R treat the omission as distance 0?', 'Apply the protocol meaning::::Omission represents infinity, not an empty numeric value.~Form the candidates::1+\\infty=\\infty,\\quad4+3=7::The omitted route offers no finite suffix.~Choose the reachable path::::Use the second neighbor at cost 7.', 'No; select the known finite route of cost 7.', 'Replacing infinity by 0 would invent a route to X that the neighbor explicitly does not advertise.'))

P('9-routing-ls', 'Link state routing', r'''
Link-state routing separates distributing topology from computing routes. A router's advertisement describes its local links; flooding and freshness rules assemble a database on which each source independently computes shortest paths. Route installation then extracts the first edge of each source path, rather than placing an entire path string in the forwarding next-hop field. Sequence numbers can distinguish newer advertisements even when older messages arrive later. These exercises assume nonnegative link costs, an agreed topology snapshot and a simple increasing sequence convention without wraparound. A shortest-path calculation is meaningful only after deciding which advertisement versions belong in that snapshot.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'The agreed undirected topology is AB=2, AC=6, BC=1, BD=5, CD=2. Compute A’s shortest route to D and next hop.', 'Improve C through B::d(B)=2,\\quad d(C)=\\min(6,2+1)=3::Reaching B offers a cheaper route than direct AC.~Compare D routes::2+5=7,\\quad3+2=5::Using C after B is best among these choices.~Extract forwarding information::::The path is A, B, C, D, but A’s installed next hop for D is B.', 'Cost 5 via path A, B, C, D; next hop B.', 'Adding 2+1+2 gives 5, while both direct candidate routes through B only or C only are larger.'),
    E('application', 'A router stores link-state advertisement from X with sequence 10: X–Y cost 2. It receives sequence 12 reporting cost 7, then delayed sequence 11 reporting cost 1. No wraparound or expiry occurs. Which cost belongs in its topology?', 'Accept the newer update::::Sequence 12 is newer than 10, so replace the stored advertisement and use cost 7.~Inspect the delayed message::::Sequence 11 is older than the currently stored 12 despite arriving later.~Preserve freshness::::Reject the stale 11 for database replacement and retain cost 7.', 'Use sequence 12 and X–Y cost 7.', 'Arrival order alone would incorrectly restore the obsolete cost 1; sequence comparison preserves the latest source update.'),
    E('trap', 'Routers A and B have the same consistent link-state database. Must their shortest-path trees have the same parent for every vertex?', 'Identify the roots::::A computes distances from A; B computes distances from B.~Construct a counterexample::::For one link A–B, A’s tree makes A the root and B its child, while B’s tree makes B the root and A its child.~Apply the distinction::::Shared topology does not make source-rooted parent relations identical.', 'No; shortest-path trees depend on the source even with identical topology knowledge.', 'Each one-edge tree is valid for its own root, and their parent assignments differ without any stale database.'))

P('9-routing-feedback-trace', 'Count to infinity', r'''
The misleading information in count-to-infinity is not just an inaccurate number but an old route's dependency on the receiver itself. Mark each advertised next hop before processing a failure. A finite metric cap can eventually stop the numerical growth, yet packet looping before convergence remains a correctness problem. Poison reverse sends an explicit unreachable advertisement back toward the neighbor used as next hop, preventing the simple two-router feedback in the stated model. It does not prevent every longer loop. These examples specify update order and the meaning of infinity so the trace is reproducible, and distinguish rejecting stale dependent routes from acquiring a genuinely independent alternative.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A–B has cost 2. Initially A reaches D directly at cost 1; B uses A with cost 3. The direct route fails and A trusts B’s stale 3. Alternate updates A then B; no protection exists. Give the first four new metrics.', 'First feedback::d_A=2+3=5::B’s stale suffix actually depended on A.~Return advertisement::d_B=2+5=7::B again selects A, retaining the same circular dependency.~Continue the sequence::d_A=2+7=9,\\quad d_B=2+9=11::No independent destination route appears.', 'New metrics 5, 7, 9, 11; both next hops still point at each other.', 'Each increase is exactly the 2-cost connecting link and no update introduces a surviving edge to D.'),
    E('application', 'Use a distance-vector cap 16 as infinity and a unit-cost A–B link. Before A’s destination failure, B advertises D=14 via A. A and B update alternately, saturating sums at 16. Find the two next updates and whether saturation restores delivery.', 'Update A::d_A=\\min(16,1+14)=15::It temporarily accepts the stale dependent route.~Update B::d_B=\\min(16,1+15)=16::The result reaches the protocol’s unreachable value.~Interpret convergence::::Saturation marks the failed route unreachable; it does not create a path or deliver buffered packets.', 'A first reports 15, then B reports 16/unreachable; delivery is not restored.', 'A’s next calculation from B’s 16 also saturates at 16, so both finally mark unreachable rather than reducing the metric to a real route.'),
    E('trap', 'B reaches D only through A. Under poison reverse, B advertises D=∞ specifically to A but advertises its finite distance elsewhere. After A loses its own D link, may A use B as a finite backup from that advertisement?', 'Find the protected interface::::A is B’s current next hop, so B poisons the advertisement sent back to A.~Evaluate the received metric::cost(A,B)+\\infty=\\infty::A sees no finite route through B.~Separate other audiences::::A finite advertisement to a different neighbor does not change the poisoned value A receives.', 'No; poison reverse blocks this two-router fallback loop.', 'The protection acts precisely on the old route’s dependency direction, rather than erasing B’s entire vector to every neighbor.'))

P('9-ipv4-cidr', 'IPv4', r'''
An IPv4 address is a thirty-two-bit identifier interpreted together with its prefix. Derive the network by retaining prefix bits and clearing host bits, then derive the upper endpoint by setting those host bits. The usual network and broadcast exclusions belong to a conventional multiaccess subnet model, rather than to every possible prefix. Point-to-point /31 links and host /32 routes deliberately use different endpoint conventions. Addresses with similar decimal spelling can belong to different networks, so use bit masks or aligned blocks. These exercises separate complete address space size from usable endpoint count and explicitly name the link model where exceptions matter.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'For 192.0.2.200/24 on a conventional IPv4 subnet, identify network, directed broadcast and usable host range.', 'Clear host bits::::A/24 fixes the first three octets, so the network is 192.0.2.0.~Set host bits::::The eight remaining bits all one give directed broadcast 192.0.2.255.~Exclude the two endpoints::::Ordinary host addresses run from 192.0.2.1 through 192.0.2.254.', 'Network 192.0.2.0; broadcast 192.0.2.255; 254 conventional host addresses.', '200 lies strictly inside 0…255, and 256 total addresses minus two endpoints gives 254.'),
    E('application', 'Find the conventional network and broadcast of 172.16.31.77/20, using the mask 255.255.240.0.', 'Mask the changing octet::31\\mathbin{\\&}240=16::The third-octet host block contains 16 consecutive values starting at 16.~Find the upper third octet::::That block ends at 31; all fourth-octet bits are host bits.~Form both endpoints::::Network 172.16.16.0; broadcast 172.16.31.255.', 'The subnet is 172.16.16.0/20, ending at 172.16.31.255.', 'Twelve host bits give 4096 addresses, matching 16 third-octet values times 256 fourth-octet values.'),
    E('trap', 'A point-to-point IPv4 link explicitly uses RFC3021 /31 semantics with prefix 192.0.2.10/31. A student subtracts network and broadcast and claims zero usable addresses. Evaluate it.', 'Count the address pair::::One host bit gives addresses 192.0.2.10 and 192.0.2.11.~Apply the stated exception::::On this /31 point-to-point link both addresses identify endpoints instead of being excluded as network/broadcast.~Reject the blanket formula::::The usual 2^h−2 expression is not the supplied endpoint convention.', 'Both .10 and .11 are usable endpoints; the zero-address conclusion is false.', 'The pair fills the two-address prefix exactly, providing one address for each point-to-point end.'))

P('9-ipv4-cidr', 'CIDR Notation', r'''
CIDR describes a power-of-two block by its fixed leading bits. Increasing the prefix length halves block size; decreasing it merges aligned sibling blocks. An aggregate is exact only when its component prefixes differ in the next bit and share all earlier bits. Two numerically adjacent networks can fail that sibling test and require a larger covering aggregate that also includes unrelated addresses. For subnet design, first count needed blocks and endpoints, choose a prefix, then enumerate aligned boundaries. The examples use total address counts unless conventional usable counts are requested, so arithmetic and address-allocation policy remain separate decisions.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Aggregate 10.4.0.0/24 and 10.4.1.0/24 into one exact CIDR prefix.', 'Compare block sizes::::Each /24 contains 256 addresses and the pair contains 512.~Check sibling alignment::::Third octets 0 and 1 differ only in their lowest bit while earlier bits agree.~Remove one prefix bit::::A/23 leaves that distinguishing bit unfixed, giving 10.4.0.0/23.', 'Exact aggregate 10.4.0.0/23.', 'Its address range 10.4.0.0…10.4.1.255 equals the two supplied blocks with no extra addresses.'),
    E('application', 'Partition 198.51.100.0/24 into four equal conventional subnets, each requiring at least 50 usable host addresses. Give the prefixes and host capacity.', 'Choose host bits::2^6-2=62\\ge50::Six host bits provide enough conventional addresses; five give only 30.~Choose the prefix::32-6=26::Each/26 occupies 64 total addresses.~Enumerate aligned starts::::Use 198.51.100.0/26, .64/26, .128/26 and.192/26.', 'Four /26 subnets, each 64 total and 62 conventional usable addresses.', 'Four 64-address blocks fill the 256-address /24 exactly, and every boundary is a multiple of 64.'),
    E('trap', 'Can 192.0.2.0/24 and 192.0.3.0/24 be aggregated exactly into 192.0.2.0/23? Then contrast 192.0.3.0/24 with 192.0.4.0/24.', 'Test the first pair::::Third octets 2 and 3 form an even-start sibling pair; 192.0.2.0/23 is exact.~Test the adjacent second pair::::Octets 3 and 4 cross a larger binary boundary and do not share the required first seven third-octet bits.~Apply alignment::::A/23 starts at an even third octet and covers that octet plus the next; none covers exactly 3 and 4.', 'The first pair aggregates exactly; the second pair does not fit one exact /23.', 'A/23 beginning at 2 includes 2, 3, while one beginning at 4 includes 4, 5; numerical adjacency alone cannot override prefix alignment.'))

P('9-ipv4-nat', 'Network Address Translation', r'''
Translation state connects an internal endpoint with its external representation. For outbound traffic, identify which fields are rewritten and which remote destination fields remain the same. Returning traffic must match the table's protocol and endpoint rules before the translator chooses an internal recipient. TCP and UDP port spaces are distinct, so the same numeric public port can describe different mappings. Unsolicited inbound access requires an explicit suitable mapping and policy; it is not supplied by knowing the public address alone. These examples model NAPT with stated mappings, no payload-embedded address rewriting and appropriate checksum updates rather than assuming NAT supplies encryption or unconditional reachability.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'NAPT maps internal TCP10.0.0.8:4500 to 203.0.113.10:60000. The client contacts 198.51.100.20:443. Show outbound and matching return endpoints.', 'Rewrite outbound source::::Source becomes 203.0.113.10:60000; destination remains 198.51.100.20:443.~Find the return mapping::::A matching reply addressed to 203.0.113.10:60000 selects the stored TCP mapping.~Restore internal destination::::The delivered reply targets 10.0.0.8:4500, with remote source 198.51.100.20:443.', 'Outbound uses the public source; return delivery restores the internal destination endpoint.', 'The remote service remains port 443 in both directions; translating the client’s source does not change the server’s endpoint.'),
    E('application', 'A translator has TCP public 203.0.113.1:4000→10.0.0.2:5000 and UDP public 203.0.113.1:4000→10.0.0.3:6000. Under protocol-aware lookup, where do matching TCP and UDP replies go?', 'Include transport identity::::The public lookup key contains protocol as well as numeric address and port.~Match TCP::::A TCP reply to public port 4000 uses the TCP entry and internal port 5000.~Match UDP::::A UDP reply at the same public port uses the UDP entry and internal port 6000.', 'TCP goes to 10.0.0.2:5000; UDP goes to 10.0.0.3:6000.', 'The two keys differ in protocol, so sharing 4000 creates no ambiguity under the stipulated lookup.'),
    E('trap', 'A NAT gateway has no entry or static forwarding for public TCP port 9000. An external host knows its public IP and sends a new connection there. Must the gateway choose any private host automatically?', 'Inspect translation state::::There is no 9000 mapping identifying an internal target.~Separate address knowledge::::The public IP identifies the gateway-facing endpoint, not a unique private application.~Apply the missing-rule outcome::::Without a suitable configured rule, no automatic recipient is implied; the packet can be rejected or dropped under policy.', 'No; public address knowledge does not establish unsolicited inbound translation.', 'Two different private hosts could both run port 9000, demonstrating why a destination-selection rule is needed.'))

P('9-ipv4-prefix', 'Longest prefix', r'''
Forwarding specificity is tested only among prefixes that actually contain the destination. First perform membership for each entry, then choose the largest prefix length. A visually similar longer prefix may not match; a default remains available only when no more specific installed route wins. Metrics can determine which candidate route is installed for a particular prefix, but an installed /32 still takes precedence over a containing /24 in this basic lookup model. Administrative policy and failed-route removal are separate supplied facts. Trace the route key and selected next hop independently, because the next-hop address is an output of lookup rather than another prefix to compare.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Routes are 0.0.0.0/0→R0, 10.0.0.0/8→R1, 10.2.0.0/16→R2, 10.3.4.0/24→R3. Forward destination 10.2.4.9.', 'Check membership::::The destination matches the default, /8 and/16 but not the 10.3.4.0/24 entry.~Compare matching lengths::16>8>0::The nonmatching/24 is excluded despite being longer.~Select the output::::Use the next hop associated with 10.2.0.0/16.', 'Next hop R2.', 'Masking the first two octets gives 10.2 for the matching /16; the other /24 begins 10.3 and cannot contain the destination.'),
    E('application', 'Installed routes 192.0.2.0/24→R1(metric 1) and 192.0.2.99/32→R2(metric 100) both match 192.0.2.99. In ordinary longest-prefix forwarding, which wins?', 'Confirm both matches::::The host belongs to the /24 and exactly equals the /32 host route.~Apply specificity::32>24::The installed host route is more specific.~Separate the metric stage::::Different metrics do not replace prefix specificity between these two installed entries.', 'Use R2 through the /32 route.', 'For 192.0.2.98 the /32 would not match and R1 would win, showing that the override is confined to the named host.'),
    E('trap', 'Route 10.0.2.0/23→R exists. Does it match 10.0.3.100 even though the third octet differs from the written network address?', 'Find the block boundary::::A/23 beginning at third octet 2 covers third octets 2 and 3.~Mask the destination::3\\mathbin{\\&}254=2::The destination’s prefix becomes 10.0.2.0.~Ignore unfixed bits::::The differing low third-octet bit is a host bit, so equality of the full printed address is unnecessary.', 'Yes; 10.0.3.100 matches 10.0.2.0/23.', 'The prefix contains 512 addresses from 10.0.2.0 through 10.0.3.255, including the test address.'))

P('9-fragmentation-fragments', 'IPv4 Fragmentation', r'''
Fragmentation partitions the original IP payload, not the entire datagram including its header. Each resulting piece receives an IP header and retains identification needed for reassembly. The MF flag describes whether original payload follows, so refragmenting a nonfinal fragment can leave MF set on every resulting subfragment. Offsets stay anchored to the original payload rather than restarting from zero at each router. A DF prohibition changes the operation from splitting to a failure response under the stated conditions. These exercises assume a fixed twenty-byte header without options, byte lengths including each header, and nonfinal payload multiples of eight.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A 1360-byte IPv4 datagram has a 20-byte header and crosses MTU620, with DF=0. Give fragment total lengths, offsets and MF flags.', 'Extract payload::1360-20=1340\\ bytes::Only payload is partitioned.~Choose aligned pieces::::The payload capacity is 600, giving 600, 600 and 140-byte pieces.~Reconstruct fields::::Total lengths 620, 620, 160; offsets 0, 75, 150; MF flags 1, 1, 0.', 'Three fragments: (620, 0, 1), (620, 75, 1), (160, 150, 0), listed as(length, offset, MF).', 'Payload sum 600+600+140=1340 and each total length is at most 620.'),
    E('application', 'An existing fragment has offset 100, payload 1600 bytes, header 20 and MF=1. Refragment across MTU820, with DF=0. Find both new offsets and MF flags.', 'Split the fragment payload::::MTU820 leaves 800 payload bytes, so two 800-byte subfragments fit.~Retain the original origin::offset_1=100,\\quad offset_2=100+800/8=200::Offsets remain relative to original payload byte 0.~Preserve original continuation::::The old MF=1 means additional original payload follows this fragment, so both new subfragments have MF=1.', 'Two 820-byte fragments with offsets 100 and 200, both MF=1.', 'They cover original payload bytes 800…1599 and 1600…2399; further original data still follows byte 2399.'),
    E('trap', 'A 1500-byte IPv4 datagram has DF=1 and reaches an outgoing link with IP MTU1000. Assume normal IPv4 forwarding and that an ICMP error can be returned. Does the router create fragments anyway?', 'Compare packet and link::::1500 exceeds the outgoing IP MTU1000.~Read the prohibition::::DF=1 disallows the ordinary router fragmentation operation.~Handle the failure::::The router drops this packet and can return fragmentation-needed feedback identifying the limiting MTU.', 'It must not fragment the datagram; it drops it and returns the permitted ICMP feedback.', 'Splitting would violate the explicit DF condition even though mathematical payload pieces could fit the link.'))

P('9-fragmentation-fragments', 'MTU', r'''
An MTU is a maximum size at a specified protocol boundary. An IP MTU includes the IP header but excludes the outer link-layer envelope; an application limit must subtract its network and transport overhead from that budget. A route's limiting MTU can be smaller than the first link's MTU. For IPv4 splitting, subtract each fragment's header and round nonfinal payload capacity down to eight-byte alignment. Do not infer one fixed application payload from MTU without naming the transport headers. These examples distinguish IP-packet budgets, resulting wire-frame sizes and fragmentation constraints using explicitly supplied field lengths.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'An IPv4 MTU is 1500. Headers are IPv420 bytes and UDP8 bytes, without options. Find the largest UDP application payload that avoids IP fragmentation.', 'Budget the whole IP packet::::The 1500 bytes include network header and UDP datagram.~Subtract both headers::1500-20-8=1472\\ bytes::These are the two headers inside the IP limit.~Check the proposed payload::::A 1472-byte application message produces a 1480-byte UDP datagram and 1500-byte IP packet.', 'Maximum payload 1472 bytes.', '1472+8+20=1500; subtracting only the IPv4 header would accidentally budget UDP’s header as application data.'),
    E('application', 'An IPv4 payload is 1000 bytes with a 20-byte header. It must cross MTU500; DF=0. Find the number and total lengths of fragments.', 'Derive aligned capacity::8\\lfloor(500-20)/8\\rfloor=480\\ bytes::Nonfinal data fits the MTU and offset alignment.~Partition payload::1000=480+480+40::Two full pieces leave a short final piece.~Add individual headers::::Total fragment lengths are 500, 500 and 60 bytes.', 'Three fragments, with total lengths 500, 500, 60.', 'The headers add 60 bytes overall, so transmitted IP bytes total 1060, while conserved original payload remains 1000.'),
    E('trap', 'An Ethernet frame contains a 1500-byte IP packet, a 14-byte Ethernet header and 4-byte FCS. Does its 1518-byte frame length violate an IP MTU of 1500?', 'Name the measured units::::The MTU here measures the enclosed IP packet, not the full Ethernet frame.~Inspect the IP length::::The packet is exactly 1500 bytes, within its stated budget.~Account for the envelope::::The extra 18 bytes belong to link framing and do not increase that packet’s IP total length.', 'No; the 1500-byte IP packet fits the stated IP MTU.', '1500+14+4=1518 describes a different layer’s unit; comparing it directly against an IP-only budget mixes boundaries.'))

P('9-fragmentation-fragments', 'Fragment offsets', r'''
The offset field locates a fragment's first data byte relative to the original payload in units of eight bytes. Headers are repeated network-layer envelopes for fragments and never accumulate into this data position. Convert an offset to a byte position before building covered intervals; add payload length to find the next uncovered byte. A correct ordinary partition is contiguous, nonoverlapping and covers the complete original payload. Overlapping fragments have special reassembly/security handling and should not be silently counted twice as distinct original bytes. The examples use zero-based byte numbering and fixed headers so offset arithmetic remains independent of header length.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A fragment offset is 23 and its payload is 128 bytes. Which original payload bytes does it cover, and what offset starts immediately after it?', 'Convert offset units::start=23(8)=184::The field counts eight-byte groups, not individual bytes.~Find the inclusive end::184+128-1=311::There are 128 covered positions starting at 184.~Locate the next piece::312/8=39::A following aligned fragment begins at byte 312.', 'Covers bytes 184…311; the immediately following offset is 39.', 'The interval length 311−184+1 equals 128, and 39−23=16 offset units equals 128 bytes.'),
    E('application', 'Three fragments have(payload length, offset, MF): (800, 0, 1), (800, 100, 1), (100, 200, 0). Verify the partition and original payload size.', 'Build intervals::::They cover 0…799, 800…1599 and 1600…1699 respectively.~Check boundary continuity::::Each start equals the previous end plus one; no gap or overlap occurs.~Use final continuation::::The last MF=0 closes the datagram, so its final byte establishes 1700 payload bytes.', 'A valid contiguous partition of 1700 original payload bytes.', '800+800+100=1700 agrees with final end 1699 plus one, independently checking the recovered size.'),
    E('trap', 'Fragments carry payload 16 at offset 0 and payload 8 at offset 1. A student says they cover 24 different original bytes. Test that statement.', 'Locate the first piece::::Offset 0 gives bytes 0…15.~Locate the second piece::1(8)=8::Its eight bytes cover 8…15, already inside the first fragment.~Count unique positions::::The union remains 0…15, only 16 distinct bytes; simple addition double-counts the overlap.', 'They do not form a nonoverlapping 24-byte partition; only 16 positions are covered.', 'The duplicate interval 8…15 accounts for all eight overcounted bytes, independent of the receiver’s chosen overlap policy.'))

P('9-tcp-web-flow', 'TCP flow control', r'''
TCP flow control protects the receiving endpoint's advertised capacity. The receive window refers to bytes relative to the acknowledgment position; already outstanding bytes consume part of the sender's allowed flight. New send allowance must also respect the congestion window, and negative remaining allowance means no ordinary new data rather than permission to send a negative amount. Sequence numbers count bytes modulo two to the thirty-second power, with SYN and FIN consuming positions under their separate rules. The examples exclude those flags when tracing pure data and distinguish an advertised zero window from permanent connection failure or an instruction to discard existing queued bytes.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A data segment begins at sequence 500, carries 300 contiguous bytes and has no SYN or FIN. The receiver has all earlier bytes and receives this segment completely. Find its cumulative ACK.', 'Number the received data::::The segment covers sequence positions 500 through 799.~Apply next-expected semantics::ACK=500+300=800::Acknowledgment names the first byte not yet received in order.~Distinguish last-byte numbering::::799 is the last delivered position, not the cumulative ACK value.', 'ACK800.', 'The inclusive interval 799−500+1 contains 300 bytes, matching the payload and next position 800.'),
    E('application', 'The sender has rwnd=12000 bytes, cwnd=8000 bytes and 7500 outstanding bytes. Ignore other sender constraints. Find additional ordinary data allowance and the effect of an ACK retiring 2000 bytes without window changes.', 'Choose the flight limit::\\min(12000,8000)=8000\\ bytes::Both receiver and network constraints must be satisfied.~Find current spare room::8000-7500=500\\ bytes::Existing unacknowledged bytes already consume space.~Retire acknowledged data::outstanding=5500,\\quad allowance=8000-5500=2500::ACK progress releases flight capacity.', 'Initially 500 bytes; after that ACK2500 bytes.', 'The allowance grows by exactly the 2000 retired bytes because both limits remain unchanged.'),
    E('trap', 'Pure TCP data starts at sequence 2³²−100 and contains 200 bytes. All arrives in order; no control flags consume sequence space. Is the next ACK2³²+100?', 'Compute the unwrapped endpoint::(2^{32}-100)+200=2^{32}+100::This arithmetic locates the next position before encoding.~Apply sequence-field width::ACK=(2^{32}+100)\\bmod2^{32}=100::The wire field wraps modulo 2³².~Preserve byte accounting::::The wrap changes the numeric representation, not the number of acknowledged bytes.', 'The encoded ACK is 100.', 'The segment spans 100 positions before the wrap and 100 after it, totaling the supplied 200 bytes.'))

P('9-tcp-web-congestion-loss', 'TCP congestion control', r'''
Congestion control is a sender's response to network feedback, while flow control represents the receiver's capacity. A congestion window in segments must be converted to bytes before combining it with a byte-valued receive window. Slow start and congestion avoidance describe different growth regimes, and a loss event's exact recovery behavior depends on the stipulated TCP variant. Work at explicit RTT or acknowledgment boundaries and keep threshold, temporary recovery window and later steady window in separate columns. These examples supply simplified recurrence rules, neglect startup transfer overhead where stated, and use window divided by RTT only as a bound on sustained useful rate.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A simplified sender starts cwnd=1 MSS and ssthresh=4 MSS. At each complete loss-free RTT below threshold, double cwnd capped at 4; once at threshold, add 1 MSS per RTT. Find cwnd after three RTTs.', 'First growth::cwnd_1=2\\ MSS::The initial window is below the threshold and follows doubling.~Reach the boundary::cwnd_2=4\\ MSS::The second RTT reaches the supplied slow-start cap.~Change the rule::cwnd_3=5\\ MSS::The third RTT starts at threshold, so congestion avoidance adds one rather than doubling.', 'Windows after RTTs 1, 2, 3 are 2, 4, 5 MSS.', 'Using exponential growth for the third RTT would incorrectly give 8 and ignore the explicitly supplied regime change.'),
    E('application', 'A long established TCP transfer has cwnd 20 MSS, MSS1460 bytes, rwnd 16 MSS and RTT40 ms. No other bottleneck or losses apply. Find the window/RTT useful-rate bound.', 'Choose the limiting window::W=\\min(20,16)1460=23360\\ bytes::The receive limit is smaller than cwnd.~Convert time::RTT=0.04\\ s::Window bytes must be divided by seconds, not milliseconds.~Calculate the rate::23360/0.04=584000\\ bytes/s=4.672\\ Mbps::Convert bytes to bits only at the final rate calculation.', 'Bound 584, 000 bytes/s, or 4.672 Mbps.', 'Sixteen 1460-byte segments per 40 ms reproduce the same rate; using cwnd alone would overestimate allowed flight.'),
    E('trap', 'Under the stipulated Reno-style rules, cwnd=12 MSS when three duplicate ACKs arrive. Set ssthresh=cwnd/2, recovery cwnd=ssthresh+3, and cwnd=ssthresh after the new-data ACK. A student resets cwnd to 1. Is that the given event’s rule?', 'Reduce the threshold::ssthresh=12/2=6\\ MSS::Use the pre-loss window as specified.~Enter and exit fast recovery::cwnd=6+3=9\\ MSS\\to6\\ MSS::The supplied duplicate-ACK path has a temporary inflated window then a six-MSS window.~Identify the mismatch::::Resetting to one belongs to a separately specified timeout reaction, not this event’s stated rules.', 'No; the specified windows are 9 during recovery and 6 after its ending ACK.', 'Neither state is 1 MSS, so applying the timeout rule contradicts the explicit duplicate-ACK contract.'))

P('9-tcp-web-local-socket-workflow', 'Socket API', r'''
The socket workflow translates an application's communication roles into distinct operating-system objects. A passive server endpoint waits for connections, while each accepted endpoint exchanges bytes with one peer. A client actively requests the server address and port. Keep object creation, local endpoint selection, connection establishment and application transfer as separate events; success at an earlier event does not prove later protocol success. In this overview, build the complete call sequence before reasoning about HTTP or DNS. The examples use ordinary blocking TCP with successful setup where explicitly stated, no inherited socket copies, and no implication that descriptor numbers are transmitted across the network.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Put these successful server operations in a valid ordinary TCP order: accept, socket, listen, bind. Explain which operation creates the connected client channel.', 'Create and name the endpoint::::socket precedes bind because a socket object must exist before receiving its local address.~Prepare passive service::::listen follows binding and marks the endpoint ready for incoming connections.~Obtain a peer channel::::accept follows listen and returns a new connected socket for one queued client.', 'socket→bind→listen→accept; accept returns the connected client socket.', 'The listener was created before any client-specific channel, and it remains distinct from the socket returned by accept.'),
    E('application', 'One TCP listener accepts three clients, returning three separate sockets. The server keeps all four objects open and a client sends a request. Which socket should receive that client’s bytes, and how many server sockets remain?', 'Count objects::1+3=4::The listener plus three accepted channels are separate server sockets.~Match the client::::The accepted socket carrying that client’s endpoint pair identifies its stream.~Choose the receive object::::Call recv on that connected socket rather than on the listener or another client’s stream.', 'Four server sockets remain; receive on the requesting client’s accepted socket.', 'Every connected socket accounts for exactly one accepted peer here, while the fourth object still serves incoming setup.'),
    E('trap', 'A blocking TCP connect returns success, but the application never sends its intended request. Has the server necessarily received an HTTP GET?', 'Locate the completed stage::::Connect success concerns establishment of the transport connection.~Look for application bytes::::The premise supplies no send and therefore no encoded GET message.~Separate stages::::An established socket cannot manufacture the missing application request.', 'No; connection success alone does not deliver an HTTP request.', 'A server waiting on recv without receiving any request bytes is consistent with the successful connect.'))

P('9-tcp-web-local-dns-workflow', 'DNS', r'''
DNS resolves typed naming questions rather than opening application connections. Before computing lookup delay, identify who performs each remaining exchange and what that participant already caches. A delegation can be cached even if the final address is absent; an address can be returned without repeating the entire hierarchy. An authoritative negative response also differs from a timeout or a host that resolved but refuses a transport connection. This overview treats naming as one explicit stage before socket setup. The examples use valid cache entries, serial exchanges when counted, and named authoritative outcomes rather than assuming every failed website request is a DNS failure.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A browser needs an IPv4 address for gate.example.test. Its recursive resolver already holds a valid A record 203.0.113.40. No policy requires revalidation. Which DNS servers must be queried upstream?', 'Identify the requested type::::The application needs an A record for the exact name.~Test cached validity::::The supplied cached A answer is still eligible for reuse.~Return without hierarchy work::::The resolver can answer directly without new root, TLD or authoritative exchanges.', 'No upstream DNS query is required for this lookup.', 'The returned typed value satisfies the request; cache reuse does not imply a TCP connection to 203.0.113.40 has been opened.'),
    E('application', 'After receiving a query, a resolver has valid delegation and server-address data for example.test but no A record for prep.example.test. One authoritative exchange takes 30 ms; repeating root and TLD would each take 20 ms. Ignore client-resolver delay. Find the required upstream time.', 'Use known delegation::::Cached authoritative location makes root and TLD discovery unnecessary.~Request the missing record::::Query the known authoritative server for prep.example.test’s A record.~Count actual exchanges::T=30\\ ms::Only the supplied missing-answer exchange is required.', '30 ms, rather than 70 ms from repeating cached discovery work.', 'The unused root/TLD costs sum 40 ms; removing that unnecessary work from 70 leaves 30.'),
    E('trap', 'An authoritative DNS server returns NXDOMAIN for a name. A student labels this as “TCP connection refused by the website.” Is that interpretation correct?', 'Identify the response protocol::::NXDOMAIN is a DNS outcome about the queried name.~Identify the naming result::::It says that the name does not exist in the relevant authoritative answer context.~Separate connection setup::::A website transport refusal would arise from a later connection attempt to an address; no such address resolution success is supplied.', 'No; this is a DNS negative naming result, not a website TCP refusal.', 'The lookup can fail before any website socket connect is attempted, so the two outcomes occur at different stages.'))

P('9-tcp-web-local-http-workflow', 'HTTP', r'''
HTTP adds request semantics and response interpretation after a usable transport channel exists. Count connection and application stages independently when building a timeline, and include discovery or security costs only when supplied. The target path tells the server which resource is requested, while the status describes the request outcome rather than the transport's health. A response body can be absent by method or status semantics, not necessarily because bytes were lost. This overview uses specified HTTP versions over TCP and simplified serial timing; the detailed HTTP lesson explores method retries, status classes, persistence and cache validation separately instead of inferring them from connection success.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A client sends GET /notes HTTP/1.1 and receives HTTP/1.1 200 OK with a representation. Identify method, target and success evidence.', 'Parse the request line::::GET is the method and /notes is the request target.~Read the response status::::200 denotes successful handling under the response semantics.~Keep the representation separate::::The response body carries content; the status is not itself the note text.', 'MethodGET; target/notes; 200 is the supplied success status.', 'The request target and response status play different roles even though they travel on the same connection.'),
    E('application', 'A tiny HTTP-over-TCP request requires one setup RTT and one request/response RTT. RTT is 25 ms. DNS costs 15 ms and TLS is absent. All stages are serial. Compute total time with a fresh connection.', 'Resolve the host::::The supplied DNS stage costs 15 ms before connecting.~Establish TCP::::Add 25 ms for the single stated setup RTT.~Exchange HTTP::T=15+25+25=65\\ ms::The request and response add another 25 ms after setup.', 'Total 65 ms.', 'DNS15 plus TCP/HTTP50 gives 65; a previously established resolved connection would omit the first 40 ms in this model.'),
    E('trap', 'A server returns a valid HTTP response with status 503. A student says no connection could have been established because the status is an error. Evaluate the claim.', 'Observe an application response::::The client received an encoded HTTP response from the server.~Interpret the class::::503 describes service unavailability at the application layer, not absence of every transport exchange.~Check the asserted contradiction::::An application error can travel over a functioning connection, so response status alone cannot prove setup failed.', 'The claim is false; 503 can be delivered over an established connection.', 'A valid received response is direct evidence that application bytes crossed the channel, even though the requested service did not succeed.'))

P('9-sockets-connection', 'Socket API', r'''
Socket descriptors are process-local handles for communication objects; their numeric values are not addresses shared with peers. A listener, accepted connection and unrelated client connection therefore require separate bookkeeping even when they share a service port. TCP supports bidirectional byte transfer on the same connected socket, with independent stream state in each direction. Closing or half-closing one channel does not automatically destroy the listener or all other peers. Trace object ownership and endpoint identity before using byte counts. These detailed exercises assume one process owns each supplied descriptor, ordinary connected TCP sockets and no descriptor inheritance or duplication that would change close behavior.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A server’s listener descriptor is 5. accept returns descriptor 9 for a client. The client’s corresponding socket descriptor is 4 in another process. Are 9 and 4 different network connections?', 'Interpret descriptor scope::::Descriptor integers identify objects within their respective processes.~Compare the supplied pairing::::The server’s 9 and client’s 4 are opposite endpoint handles for the stated accepted connection.~Keep the listener distinct::::Server descriptor 5 remains its passive listener, not an extra copy of the peer’s descriptor 4.', 'No; 9 and 4 can name opposite ends of the same connection; 5 is the listener.', 'Network identity comes from protocol and endpoint tuples, so unrelated process-local descriptor numbers need not match.'),
    E('application', 'A server has listener 3 and connected sockets 4 and 5 for two clients. Client 1’s connection on 4 completes and server closes 4. It then accepts client 3 into 6. Find the open server socket set and affected clients.', 'Close only the supplied channel::::Removing 4 ends the server’s handle for client 1; 3 and 5 stay open.~Accept the new peer::::Listener 3 returns the independent connected socket 6.~Enumerate the survivors::::The open set is{3, 5, 6}; client 2 on 5 and client 3 on 6 are unaffected by closing 4.', 'Open descriptors 3, 5, 6; the listener and the other two clients remain usable.', 'Two connected sockets plus one listener again total three; closure was scoped to one owned descriptor.'),
    E('trap', 'A connected TCP socket is used for a request and a reply. A student proposes opening a second connection solely because send and recv must use different sockets. Is that an API requirement?', 'Identify duplex capability::::A connected TCP channel carries byte streams in both directions.~Assign the operations::::The same connected socket can send the local request and receive the remote reply.~Separate optional design::::An application may choose multiple channels, but direction alone does not require it.', 'No; send and recv may use the same connected TCP socket.', 'An ordinary request/response exchange on one persistent connection is a counterexample to the proposed requirement.'))

P('9-sockets-bind-endpoint', 'bind', r'''
Binding chooses the local part of a socket endpoint, and the operating system checks whether that choice is permissible and available. A zero port requests allocation of a free ephemeral port; the application must discover the selected value instead of treating zero as its final public service port. Wildcard and specific-address binds cover different local address sets and can conflict under supplied exclusivity rules. Binding a TCP socket does not itself make it a listener or connect it to a remote endpoint. The examples explicitly exclude reuse options and rely on stated platform behavior, because real bind-conflict details vary across address families and socket configurations.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'An unconnected TCP socket binds to 192.0.2.8:0. The operating system selects port 52000. Identify its local and remote endpoint information immediately afterward.', 'Apply port-zero semantics::::Zero requested an available local port; the selected value is 52000.~Record the binding::::The socket’s actual local endpoint is 192.0.2.8:52000.~Inspect remote state::::No connect or accept has occurred, so no remote TCP peer is established yet.', 'Local endpoint 192.0.2.8:52000; no established remote endpoint.', 'The chosen nonzero port, rather than the requested zero, is what later local endpoint inspection reports.'),
    E('application', 'Under stated exclusive-bind rules with no reuse options, a TCP listener binds wildcard 0.0.0.0:8080 across local IPv4 interfaces. Can another socket bind 192.0.2.5:8080? Could it instead bind 192.0.2.5:8081?', 'Interpret wildcard coverage::::The first binding already covers 192.0.2.5 at port 8080 under the supplied rule.~Test the same service port::::The second 8080 endpoint overlaps and its bind fails.~Change the endpoint key::::Port 8081 is a distinct available local endpoint, so the alternative can bind.', '8080 conflicts; 8081 can bind if otherwise free.', 'The specific interface does not escape the wildcard’s stated address coverage, while changing port avoids that overlap.'),
    E('trap', 'A stream socket has successfully bound to a service address but has never called listen. May a server treat bind success as proof it can call accept on a listening endpoint?', 'Name what bind did::::It assigned a local address and port.~Check passive state::::The premise contains no listen transition preparing a connection queue.~Evaluate the proposed accept::::Bind alone does not establish the required listening state for ordinary TCP accept.', 'No; bind success is not listen success.', 'The normal sequence socket→bind→listen→accept contains a separate state-changing operation omitted by the proposal.'))

P('9-sockets-connection', 'listen', r'''
Listen prepares a stream socket for passive connection establishment and maintains a queue according to the operating system's specified backlog behavior. It does not return a per-client connected socket or receive an application request. Backlog concerns pending connection state under a supplied model, not the total number of already accepted clients the server can ever serve. Exact queue counts differ by platform, so numerical exercises must define whether they count completed pending connections and how overflow is handled. Once accept removes an entry, pending capacity can become available while the accepted channel continues independently. The exercises use an explicit completed-connection queue model without guessing implementation details.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A bound TCP socket successfully calls listen. No client has connected and accept has not run. Identify its role and whether a connected peer socket has been returned.', 'Observe the state transition::::The bound socket is now a passive listening endpoint.~Inspect pending peers::::The supplied queue has no client connections yet.~Separate accept’s output::::Listen has not returned an accepted connected socket; that is a later operation.', 'It is a listener with no accepted client socket yet.', 'Successful readiness for connections does not create a remote endpoint without an actual client establishment.'),
    E('application', 'A simplified listen queue holds at most two completed pending connections. C1 and C2 finish setup; C3’s completion is deferred while the queue is full. The server acceptsC1, thenC3 may complete. Trace queue contents.', 'Fill the pending queue::::After C1 and C2 it contains[C1, C2]; C3 is not admitted by the given overflow rule.~Accept one peer::::Removing C1 leaves[C2], opening one pending slot.~Admit the deferred completion::::C3 can now enter, giving[C2, C3], while acceptedC1 continues on its own socket.', 'Final pending queue[C2, C3]; C1 is an independent accepted connection.', 'Two pending entries satisfy the capacity, and the accepted connection is not counted as still occupying a pending slot.'),
    E('trap', 'In the same completed-pending queue model, backlog is 2 and a server already has 100 accepted clients with no pending arrivals. Must listen reject every new client because 100>2?', 'Identify what backlog counts::::It bounds the stipulated pending queue, not the accepted client population.~Inspect the current queue::::No arrivals are pending, so both queue slots are free.~Apply the correct limit::::A new completed arrival can enter the queue, subject to other resources excluded from the question.', 'No; backlog 2 does not mean a lifetime maximum of two accepted clients.', 'The 100 accepted connections have already been removed from this queue, so comparing them directly with its capacity uses the wrong count.'))

P('9-sockets-connection', 'accept', r'''
Accept transfers one queued connection into an application-visible connected socket and reports the peer information under the API. The original listener remains passive, allowing subsequent accepts. The returned channel uses the server's local service endpoint with a particular remote endpoint, so multiple accepted sockets can share a local port without being the same connection. Depending on blocking mode, an empty queue can cause waiting or a would-block result, rather than producing a fake client socket. The exercises assume successful completed TCP setup when a queued client is supplied and explicit blocking behavior otherwise, with no descriptor duplication or inherited references.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Listener 10 at 192.0.2.1:9000 has queued peer 198.51.100.8:53000. accept returns descriptor 11. Give the connected endpoint pair and listener’s remaining role.', 'Retain the service endpoint::::Socket 11 has local 192.0.2.1:9000.~Attach the peer::::Its remote endpoint is 198.51.100.8:53000 from the queued connection.~Preserve the passive object::::Descriptor 10 remains the listener and can accept later clients.', '11 is the connected channel for the supplied pair; 10 remains listening.', 'The returned descriptor differs from 10 while its local service port remains 9000, demonstrating object versus endpoint distinctions.'),
    E('application', 'A listener has queued connections from A:5000 and B:5000, where A and B are different IP addresses. It successfully accepts both without closing anything. Can the two accepted sockets be distinguished despite equal remote ports?', 'Keep the full remote endpoint::::A:5000 and B:5000 differ in IP address.~Combine both endpoint pairs::::Each shares the server local endpoint but has a different remote address/port tuple.~Assign separate channels::::Two accept calls return separate connected sockets, each retaining its own peer identity.', 'Yes; different remote IP addresses distinguish the accepted connections.', 'Equal source-port numbers do not make full TCP endpoint tuples equal; omitting the address would discard necessary identity information.'),
    E('trap', 'A listener’s queue is empty and it uses blocking accept with no timeout. A student expects an immediate connected socket with remote address 0.0.0.0. What actually follows from the model?', 'Check available connections::::There is no queued peer to transfer.~Apply blocking semantics::::The operation waits until a connection arrives or another specified interrupt/error occurs.~Reject invented metadata::::The API does not fabricate a connected peer merely to return immediately.', 'It blocks under these assumptions; no synthetic 0.0.0.0 peer is returned.', 'A connected socket requires a real completed connection, and the stipulated empty queue supplies none.'))

P('9-sockets-connect-endpoint', 'connect', r'''
Connect has protocol-specific meaning. For a blocking TCP socket, successful completion establishes transport state with a remote endpoint under the supplied model. For a UDP socket, a connect operation can merely set a default destination and restrict accepted peer traffic; it does not perform TCP's handshake or transform UDP into a reliable byte stream. An unbound client can receive a selected local endpoint, and a setup error must be interpreted before expecting any application status. The examples explicitly name TCP or UDP and avoid treating a successful system call as proof that the peer application has processed a request or that later traffic cannot fail.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'An unbound blocking TCP client calls connect to 203.0.113.7:443. The OS selects local 192.0.2.4:54000 and setup succeeds. Identify each endpoint’s role.', 'Record the selected local endpoint::::192.0.2.4:54000 belongs to the client’s side.~Record the requested peer::::203.0.113.7:443 is the remote server endpoint.~Interpret success::::TCP setup is complete in this model, but no application message is supplied yet.', 'Client local 192.0.2.4:54000; remote 203.0.113.7:443.', 'The service port 443 does not become the client’s source port; the OS-selected 54000 keeps the two roles distinct.'),
    E('application', 'A blocking TCP connect to a host’s closed port returns a connection-refused error before any request bytes are sent. Should an application interpret this as HTTP404 or skip checking connect’s result?', 'Locate failure time::::The transport setup attempt failed before an HTTP request exchange.~Read the available evidence::::There is a socket error, not a received HTTP status line.~Choose application handling::::Handle the setup failure; do not read a fictional 404 response from a channel that never established.', 'Handle the connect error; it is not HTTP404.', 'A 404 requires an application response, whereas the supplied failure precedes every request byte.'),
    E('trap', 'A POSIX-style UDP connect sets default peer 203.0.113.9:53 without a transport handshake. Does success prove a reliable connection or even that the remote DNS server is running?', 'Use the named transport::::The operation applies to UDP, not TCP.~Apply the stated contract::::The local socket stores peer information without a handshake proving remote availability.~Limit the inference::::Success cannot guarantee delivery, reliability or a running remote application.', 'No; it establishes local UDP peer settings only under the supplied semantics.', 'An offline server can coexist with successful local UDP connect because no response was required.'))

P('9-sockets-stream', 'send', r'''
Send reports how many supplied bytes the local operation accepted under its API, so the application advances its buffer position by that returned positive count. A short write needs continuation from the first unsent byte, not repetition of the whole message. Even a complete successful send does not establish that the remote application has consumed the content; TCP acknowledgment and application-level success are separate evidence. Define framing before transmitting because stream sends do not create durable message boundaries at the receiver. These exercises assume positive return counts or a stated error, no signal interruption and a specified byte sequence, leaving nonblocking readiness handling to its supplied protocol.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Send the eight bytesABCDEFGH. Consecutive send calls accept 3, 2 and 3 bytes. Show the argument suffix before each call.', 'First progress::::The first call suppliesABCDEFGH and acceptsABC.~Continue after returned count::::The next suffix isDEFGH; acceptingDE advances total progress to 5.~Finish the remaining bytes::::SupplyFGH for the third call, which accepts all 3.', 'Call suffixesABCDEFGH, DEFGH, FGH; the accepted stream isABCDEFGH.', '3+2+3=8 and no already accepted prefix is resent or skipped.'),
    E('application', 'A protocol sends a two-byte big-endian length prefix 0006 followed by six payload bytesABCDEF. Each successful send accepts exactly min(3, remaining) bytes with no errors. List its transmitted pieces.', 'Construct the framed buffer::::The eight bytes are 00, 06, A, B, C, D, E, F; the prefix counts payload only.~Apply first two partial writes::::The first call accepts 00, 06, A; the second acceptsB, C, D.~Complete the loop::::The final call acceptsE, F, and all eight bytes have been queued in order.', 'Three pieces:[00, 06, A], [B, C, D], [E, F].', 'The receiver can reconstruct prefix 6 and six payload bytes regardless of these three call boundaries.'),
    E('trap', 'send returns 4 after the local program supplies four request bytes. The remote application is paused and has not read its socket. Does return 4 prove the remote request was processed?', 'Interpret the return count::::All four supplied bytes were accepted by the local send operation.~Inspect remote application state::::The premise says the peer has not read them yet.~Separate acknowledgment layers::::Local send completion is not evidence of application processing or a successful response.', 'No; four accepted bytes do not prove remote processing.', 'The paused receiver is an explicit counterexample consistent with a successful local send.'))

P('9-sockets-stream', 'recv', r'''
Recv gives the bytes available under its stream-read semantics, up to the caller's capacity, rather than guaranteeing the application record's complete size. Maintain a receive buffer and parser state separately: first collect a header, then validate its declared length, then collect exactly that payload, preserving any subsequent bytes. A positive short read is progress; zero denotes orderly end in the stated TCP model. End of stream does not retroactively complete an undersized framed message. These exercises use byte streams with explicit framing, no nonblocking errors and zero-return EOF semantics, so protocol completeness can be decided from accumulated bytes rather than call boundaries.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A fixed-size application record has 8 bytes. recv is called with capacity 16 but returns 3 bytes, then 5. No EOF or errors occur. When is the record complete?', 'Respect the actual first count::::Store the 3 returned bytes; buffer capacity 16 does not mean 16 arrived.~Compute the unmet record length::8-3=5::Five further bytes are required by the protocol.~Append the second result::::The next 5 bytes complete the record without discarding the first prefix.', 'Complete after the second read, with 8 accumulated bytes.', '3+5 equals the fixed record size; the 16-byte request capacity was merely an upper limit on one read.'),
    E('application', 'A two-byte big-endian length prefix declares payload 5. recv returns chunks[00], [05, h, e], [l, l, o]. Parse the complete message.', 'Collect the header::::The first chunk has only one prefix byte, so wait for the second byte rather than reading a length prematurely.~Finish the header and start payload::::The second chunk completes length 0005 and provides payloadh, e, two of the five bytes.~Complete payload::::The final chunk suppliesl, l, o; combined content ishello.', 'One complete five-byte messagehello.', 'Two header bytes plus five payload bytes total seven received bytes across chunks of 1, 3 and 3.'),
    E('trap', 'A protocol declares a 5-byte payload. The receiver has accumulated 2 payload bytes, then recv returns 0 on the TCP stream. Can it treat the 2-byte prefix as a complete message?', 'Interpret zero::::The specified zero return signals orderly end of stream, no additional payload bytes.~Check protocol completeness::5-2=3\\ bytes::Three declared bytes are still missing.~Report the framing outcome::::The transport closed before completing the record; the partial content is not a valid five-byte message.', 'No; this is a truncated framed message despite orderly TCP closure.', 'A normal complete record would contain five payload bytes before EOF; the length check independently exposes the missing three.'))

P('9-dns-resolution', 'DNS', r'''
DNS's hierarchy delegates authority over name suffixes. A root referral identifies the next naming service; a top-level-domain referral narrows the authority further; a zone's authoritative server supplies records within its responsibility. A lookup is also typed: discovering a name's mail service can require MX data and subsequent address lookups, rather than returning the website's A record. A CNAME links names and can add another resolution step. Trace names, types and responsible servers separately, while recognizing that caches can bypass discovery work. These detailed examples assume valid delegations, known referral-server addresses where needed and authoritative answers without treating a referral itself as a final host address.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Resolve an uncached A query for study.example.com. Root refers to .com; the .com server refers to example.com; the example.com authoritative server returns 203.0.113.50. Identify each answer’s role.', 'Read the root response::::It gives a .com delegation, not study.example.com’s address.~Read the TLD response::::It identifies the authoritative service for example.com, still not the final A value.~Use the authoritative record::::The final response supplies the requested IPv4 address 203.0.113.50.', 'Two discovery referrals followed by the final authoritative A answer.', 'Only the last supplied record has the requested name/type meaning; nameserver location data do not replace it.'),
    E('application', 'A mail sender queries example.test MX and receives preference 10 mx.example.test. The latter name has A 203.0.113.60. Identify what must be resolved before connecting to the selected mail host; do not count protocol-specific port selection as DNS data.', 'Interpret the MX record::::It chooses a mail-exchanger name rather than directly providing an IP address.~Resolve the target type::::Query or reuse the A record for mx.example.test to obtain 203.0.113.60.~Separate connection setup::::Use the resolved address with the mail application’s selected service port; DNS lookup itself does not open that connection.', 'Resolve mx.example.test’s address before connecting; the IPv4 target is 203.0.113.60.', 'The MX payload is a name and preference, while the A payload is the numeric endpoint address, so both record roles are necessary.'),
    E('trap', 'An authoritative answer says www.example.test CNAME edge.example.test, with no address record supplied. May an IPv4 client treat the characters edge.example.test as an IPv4 address?', 'Identify the returned type::::CNAME states an alias target in the naming system.~Check the required type::::The client needs numeric IPv4 data from an A record, not another domain-name string.~Continue resolution::::Follow the alias and obtain the target’s A answer or its authoritative negative result.', 'No; the alias target still needs address resolution.', 'A dotted DNS name can contain alphabetic labels and is not the four-octet numeric value carried by an A record.'))

P('9-dns-resolution', 'Recursive resolution', r'''
Recursive service assigns completion of a naming request to the responding resolver. The stub asks for the final eligible answer, while the resolver handles cached data, referrals and additional required queries on its behalf. That resolver may use iterative upstream exchanges, so recursion at the client boundary does not mean every server recursively contacts the next one. For timing, one held client request can encompass several serial upstream operations; do not charge the client-resolver round trip again for every referral. The examples define cache state and include or exclude network legs explicitly, distinguishing the service contract from the number of packets observed.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A stub sends one request for recursive A resolution to R. R has no cached answer and follows root and TLD referrals before obtaining the authoritative result. Who follows those referrals and who returns the final answer to the stub?', 'Assign the requested service::::The stub delegates completion of the query to R.~Follow upstream knowledge::::R performs the referral-following exchanges on the stub’s behalf.~Complete the original request::::R returns the final eligible A answer rather than requiring the stub to contact each referred server.', 'R follows the referrals and answers the stub’s original recursive request.', 'One stub-resolver service relationship can contain several iterative upstream queries without changing who owns completion.'),
    E('application', 'A recursive query travels 3 ms from stub to resolver. The resolver then performs three serial uncached exchanges of 15, 25 and 10 ms. Returning the final answer takes 3 ms. Ignore processing and all other delays. Find the stub’s elapsed time.', 'Add upstream work::15+25+10=50\\ ms::The resolver performs these exchanges sequentially.~Add client network legs::3+3=6\\ ms::The original query and final response each cross the stub-resolver path once.~Combine elapsed stages::T=50+6=56\\ ms::No intermediate referral is sent back for the stub to resolve.', '56 ms.', 'The timeline 0→3→53→56 separately locates request arrival, upstream completion and final delivery.'),
    E('trap', 'A resolver immediately answers a recursive query from its valid cache, without contacting root. A student claims the exchange was iterative because it used only one query. Is packet count decisive?', 'Identify responsibility::::The client asked the resolver to complete the lookup.~Inspect completion::::The resolver supplied the final answer itself using eligible stored data.~Reject the packet-count test::::No referral-following burden was returned to the client; one exchange can still provide recursive service.', 'No; this is still recursive service at the client-resolver boundary.', 'Adding or removing cache hits changes work and timing, not the request’s completion contract.'))

P('9-dns-resolution', 'Iterative resolution', r'''
An iterative request asks a server to supply what it can answer or identify the next authority, leaving further work to the requester. A referral is progress rather than the desired final host record. The requester must track the name and type across each delegation and acquire the next server's address if it is not already known. An authoritative server may supply a complete answer in the first iterative exchange; iterative does not mean that referrals must always occur. The examples provide referral-server addresses when counting simple RTTs, exclude aliases unless named, and make the serial ordering explicit instead of silently assuming parallel work.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Resolver R performs iterative queries. Root refers it to TLD server T, which refers it to authoritative server A. Addresses of T and A are supplied. What is R’s next action after each referral?', 'Handle the root referral::::R contacts T itself; root is not required to finish the host lookup.~Handle the TLD referral::::R then contacts A using the supplied location.~Reach a complete answer::::A’s authoritative response can satisfy the original requested record.', 'R follows root→T→A and obtains the final answer from A.', 'Each referral changes the server queried while the original target name/type remains the resolution goal.'),
    E('application', 'An uncached iterative lookup uses one root exchange 12 ms, one TLD exchange 18 ms and one authoritative exchange 30 ms. All server addresses are known and exchanges are serial. Find elapsed upstream time and number of exchanges.', 'Count discovery stages::::Root and TLD contribute two referral exchanges.~Count the answer stage::::One authoritative exchange supplies the final record; there is no fourth confirmation query.~Add serial RTTs::12+18+30=60\\ ms::Each complete exchange finishes before the next starts.', 'Three exchanges totaling 60 ms.', 'The two referral delays sum 30 ms and the final answer adds another 30; the exchange count matches the three named servers.'),
    E('trap', 'A requester sends an iterative A query directly to the correct authoritative server, which returns a complete A answer. Must the server instead return a referral because the query was iterative?', 'Identify local authority::::The contacted server can answer the requested record authoritatively.~Apply iterative semantics::::It may supply the information it already has; recursion is not necessary.~Check for remaining work::::A complete answer removes any need for a further referral.', 'No; an iterative query can receive a complete authoritative answer.', 'Referrals are needed when the contacted server cannot finish from its own authority or eligible knowledge, not because every iterative exchange requires one.'))

P('9-dns-records', 'TTL', r'''
TTL limits the eligible reuse of a particular cached record as its age increases. Set an expiry when the response is stored under the given lifetime, then compare each later request with that expiry. Reading a cached record is not a new authoritative response and therefore does not reset its original lifetime. A later genuine refresh can establish a new lifetime, possibly different from the old one. Distinct records in a resolution chain can expire separately. The exercises assume no eviction, clock adjustments or special stale-serving policy, and count expiry from the supplied response time rather than from a human date or the latest client access.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'An A record is stored at t=20 s with TTL90 s. At t=70, find age, remaining lifetime and expiry time.', 'Compute record age::70-20=50\\ s::Only time since storage consumes the supplied lifetime.~Compute remaining budget::90-50=40\\ s::The record is still eligible before ordinary expiry.~Locate expiry::20+90=110\\ s::All reuse decisions refer to this original deadline.', 'Age 50 s; remaining 40 s; expiry t=110 s.', 'Current time 70 plus remaining 40 equals expiry 110, independently checking the subtraction.'),
    E('application', 'An answer stored at t=0 has TTL60. Query t=30 uses it. At t=75 an expired lookup obtains a new authoritative answer with TTL120. No other refresh occurs. Find expiry and remaining lifetime at t=150.', 'Preserve the first expiry::::The t=30 read does not change the original t=60 deadline.~Establish the refreshed lifetime::expiry_{new}=75+120=195\\ s::The real new answer creates a new storage origin.~Check the later query::195-150=45\\ s::The refreshed entry is valid with 45 seconds remaining.', 'New expiry t=195; remaining 45 s at t=150.', 'Its age is 150−75=75, and 120−75=45 agrees with the expiry calculation.'),
    E('trap', 'TTL10 is stored at t=0. A client reads it at t=9 and again at t=12. No fresh upstream answer occurs. A student resets its expiry to 19 after the first read. Evaluate the second reuse.', 'Keep the storage origin::::Ordinary cache access at 9 does not replace the source response.~Apply the true deadline::expiry=0+10=10::The record has already expired by 12.~Reject the reset::::A deadline of 19 would require a new lifetime event that the premise excludes.', 'Ordinary reuse at t=12 is invalid; the entry expired at 10.', 'Age 12 exceeds TTL10 even though the last client access was only 3 seconds earlier.'))

P('9-dns-record-chain', 'Resource records', r'''
The type field determines how to interpret DNS data. A and AAAA give numeric addresses of different families; MX names an exchanger with a preference; NS names an authority; CNAME links an alias to a target name. Equal-looking name strings in these fields do not imply equal protocol behavior. Follow record-specific rules before issuing the next query, including selecting the preferred exchanger and resolving its address. A referral's nameserver does not thereby become the requested application's endpoint. The examples use supplied record sets with no ties or unavailable servers, and separate the choice of a target name from the address needed to contact it.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Records are host.example A 203.0.113.8, host.example AAAA2001:db8::8, and example MX10 mail.example. Identify which records directly give IPv4, IPv6 and a mail-service name.', 'Read A semantics::::203.0.113.8 is the supplied IPv4 address.~Read AAAA semantics::::2001:db8::8 is the supplied IPv6 address.~Read MX semantics::::mail.example is an exchanger name with preference 10, not a numeric address.', 'A→IPv4; AAAA→IPv6; MX→mail target name and preference.', 'Only the address records carry numeric host addresses; the MX name may need an additional typed lookup.'),
    E('application', 'example.test has MX10 mail1.example.test and MX20 mail2.example.test. Both are available; lower preference wins. Their A addresses are 203.0.113.10 and 203.0.113.20. Which address is the initial mail target?', 'Compare preferences::10<20::The stipulated selection favors mail1.~Resolve the chosen name::::mail1.example.test’s A record supplies 203.0.113.10.~Separate fallback::::mail2 is not the initial target under the given availability assumption.', 'Initial target 203.0.113.10 through mail1.example.test.', 'The preference chooses a name first; matching that name’s A record yields the address without mistaking 10 for a port or IP value.'),
    E('trap', 'A delegation contains example.test NS ns.provider.test. A student interprets it as example.test CNAME ns.provider.test and sends the website request to the nameserver. What is wrong?', 'Interpret NS::::It identifies a server responsible for DNS authority or delegation handling.~Distinguish CNAME::::An alias record would map the queried name to another target name; no such record was supplied.~Continue the right query::::Ask the nameserver for the required website address record rather than substituting its own host as the website.', 'NS is authority information, not a website alias.', 'The nameserver can host a zone for a website located at an entirely different address, so these endpoints need not coincide.'))

P('9-http-semantics', 'HTTP', r'''
HTTP names resources at the application layer and lets a server interpret request method, target and authority. Several websites can share a network address while choosing different representations based on their HTTP authority information. A body's absence can be intentional, especially for HEAD or statuses that forbid a representation body; its metadata need not imply that transmission lost content. Version matters when reasoning about the transport:HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 commonly use TCP, whileHTTP/3 uses QUIC. The examples state the version and resource-selection rules, excluding TLS routing complexity unless specified, so application semantics are not guessed solely from an IP address or a TCP connection.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A HEAD request succeeds with status 200 and Content-Length:400, but no response body. Does that necessarily indicate 400 lost bytes?', 'Apply HEAD semantics::::HEAD requests the metadata corresponding to retrieval without transferring the representation body.~Interpret the header::::Content-Length can describe the representation length that a corresponding GET would send.~Check protocol consistency::::The absent body is intentional under HEAD rather than evidence of loss.', 'No; this can be a valid 400-byte representation’s metadata-only response.', 'The method explicitly excludes the retrieval body, so comparing received body 0 against 400 without reading the method is invalid.'),
    E('application', 'An HTTP/1.1 server at one IP hosts exam.example and blog.example. Its routing uses Host plus request target. Compare GET / on connections to the same IP with Host:exam.example and Host:blog.example.', 'Hold the network endpoint fixed::::Both requests reach the same server address.~Read application authority::::The two Host fields name different virtual hosts.~Select representations::::The stipulated HTTP router may return the exam home page for one and blog home page for the other.', 'Same IP and path can produce different resources because Host differs.', 'The routing key includes both authority and target; matching only the IP would discard one supplied selector.'),
    E('trap', 'An application uses HTTP/3. A student mechanically adds a TCP three-way-handshake RTT to every request because “HTTP always uses TCP.” Assess the assumption.', 'Identify the version::::HTTP/3 uses QUIC rather than the HTTP-over-TCP setup assumed by that formula.~Separate setup models::::QUIC/security and reuse behavior require their own stated timing facts.~Reject the universal addition::::A TCP handshake cost is not automatically an HTTP/3 stage.', 'The assumption is false; use the supplied HTTP/3/QUIC timing model.', 'A protocol stack without TCP cannot require a TCP handshake solely because its application protocol is HTTP.'))

P('9-http-method-retry', 'Methods', r'''
Method properties describe intended effects, not a guarantee that each response is identical. Safe methods request observation rather than state mutation; idempotent methods preserve the same intended effect when repeated. DELETE can return different statuses after its first removal while remaining idempotent. An endpoint can also impose a deduplication contract on POST with an idempotency key, so concrete retry behavior needs both method semantics and the application's stated rules. Evaluate the final resource state after each processing event, and distinguish that state from audit logs or response codes. These exercises concern intended resource effects and explicitly supplied deduplication rather than accidental server implementations.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Classify ordinary GET and HEAD by safety and idempotence under their intended HTTP semantics. HEAD requests only metadata.', 'Identify retrieval effects::::Both request information rather than intended resource mutation.~Repeat the requests::::Repeating retrieval leaves the intended resource state unchanged.~Apply both labels::::They are safe and idempotent under the named semantics, even if returned information evolves independently.', 'GET and HEAD are both safe and idempotent.', 'A changing clock value in a response does not itself make retrieval mutate the requested resource or violate intended-effect idempotence.'),
    E('application', 'DELETE /draft/7 removes an existing draft and returns 204. An identical retry finds it absent and returns 404. No recreation occurs. Did differing responses make the operation nonidempotent?', 'Apply the first effect::::Draft 7 changes from present to absent.~Apply the retry effect::::It remains absent; the intended deletion effect does not accumulate.~Compare state rather than status::::204 and 404 report different circumstances while both executions leave the desired absent state.', 'No; the stipulated DELETE effect is idempotent despite 204 then 404.', 'Applying the operation twice yields the same final draft state as applying it once.'),
    E('trap', 'A POST /jobs endpoint records an idempotency key. For the same key and identical payload, it returns the original job instead of creating another. Two retries carry keyK. Must three jobs exist because POST is generally nonidempotent?', 'Read the endpoint contract::::The supplied key uniquely deduplicates this operation.~Apply first processing::::One job is created and associated with K.~Apply retries::::Both matchK and the same payload, so both reuse the original job.', 'Only one job exists under this explicit deduplication contract.', 'Without the key contract repeated creation could occur; the stated application rule is the decisive added information here.'))

P('9-http-semantics', 'Status codes', r'''
Status codes summarize outcomes of received application responses. The first digit separates broad classes, while the particular code supplies the meaning needed for handling. A redirect can ask a client to make a new request at another target; it is not the new target's eventual success response. A not-modified response relies on a prior cached representation and deliberately omits a new body. Treat a code as evidence about the supplied request rather than as a diagnosis of every network stage. The examples use explicit statuses and redirect/cache context, avoiding the inference that all non-2 xx outcomes are equivalent or that transport failure produces an HTTP status.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'GET /old receives 301 with Location:/new. Following the redirect produces 200 for GET /new. Identify how many application requests occurred and which response supplied final success.', 'Read the first outcome::::301 directs the client to another target rather than supplying final 200 success.~Follow the new target::::The client sends a second request for/new.~Identify completion::::The second response 200 represents successful handling of that new request.', 'Two requests; the 200 response to/new supplies final success.', 'The Location value is an instruction for another target, not proof that the first request already received the final representation.'),
    E('application', 'Three received responses are 200, 404 and 503. Classify them by status class and distinguish resource absence from temporary service unavailability.', 'Classify success::::200 belongs to 2 xx and represents supplied successful handling.~Classify the target outcome::::404 belongs to 4 xx and indicates the requested target was not found under that response.~Classify server availability::::503 belongs to 5 xx and describes unavailable service, not necessarily an absent target.', '200:success; 404:client-related not-found outcome; 503:server-related service unavailability.', 'Their leading digits 2, 4 and 5 reproduce the classes, but the specific 04 and 03 meanings distinguish the two failures.'),
    E('trap', 'A conditional GET receives 304 Not Modified. The client already stores the matching body. A student treats all 3 xx as a failed download needing the empty response body displayed as the document. What should happen?', 'Read the specific code::::304 validates a stored representation rather than redirecting to a new page.~Use the supplied cache state::::The matching cached body remains the representation to use.~Separate response body rules::::An absent new body is expected and should not erase the stored content.', 'Reuse the validated cached representation; do not replace it with an empty document.', 'The body already exists locally; 304’s purpose here is to avoid transmitting it again while confirming validity.'))

P('9-http-reuse', 'Persistence', r'''
Persistence reuses a transport connection and can remove repeated setup, while request ordering determines whether application round trips overlap. HTTP/1.1 can reuse a connection yet issue sequential requests; pipelining sends several requests before their responses, with response ordering constraints. HTTP/2 adds stream multiplexing but still has underlying transport and server limits. Build a timeline from when requests reach the server, how it processes them, and when replies return. The numerical examples explicitly omit transfer and security costs and name a simplified processing schedule. Connection reuse is therefore one saved stage, not proof that all work completes instantly or that concurrent streams escape every shared bottleneck.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Two tiny objects use HTTP over TCP. A new connection costs one RTT and each sequential request/response costs one RTT. Compare separate new connections with one new persistent connection; ignore all other work.', 'Count independent setup::::Two fresh connections each pay setup plus exchange, giving 2+2=4 RTT.~Reuse one setup::::A persistent connection pays setup once for both objects.~Count both exchanges::::Sequential requests still require two exchange RTTs, so total is 1+2=3 RTT.', 'Separate connections 4 RTT; persistent reuse 3 RTT.', 'The one-RTT difference is exactly the removed second setup; neither application exchange disappears.'),
    E('application', 'On an established HTTP/1.1 connection, eight pipelined tiny requests are sent together. One-way propagation is 25 ms; server processes them sequentially for 5 ms each and sends each tiny response immediately. Ignore transfer. Find the last response arrival and compare eight sequential exchanges.', 'Reach the server::::All pipelined requests arrive at 25 ms under the zero-transfer assumption.~Finish ordered work::25+8(5)=65\\ ms::The server’s final response is ready after forty milliseconds of processing.~Return the final response::65+25=90\\ ms::Sequential exchanges would each cost 50+5=55 ms, total 440 ms.', 'Pipelined final arrival 90 ms; sequential total 440 ms in this model.', 'The 90 ms total is one round trip 50 plus all server work 40, whereas sequential operation pays the propagation round trip eight times.'),
    E('trap', 'An established HTTP/2 connection multiplexes several streams over TCP. A student concludes the streams never share delay from TCP byte loss. Is that guaranteed?', 'Identify the common transport::::The streams share one underlying ordered TCP byte sequence.~Consider a missing transport byte::::TCP can withhold later bytes until the gap is repaired, even if those later bytes encode different HTTP streams.~Limit the claim::::Application multiplexing does not eliminate every cross-stream delay caused by that common transport.', 'No; HTTP/2 multiplexing over TCP does not guarantee absence of transport head-of-line delay.', 'One missing earlier TCP byte followed by later stream data is a counterexample consistent with application-level multiplexing.'))

P('9-http-cache-validation', 'Caching', r'''
Caching combines storage permission, freshness and validation rules. A stored response may be private to one user's cache, while shared caches need an appropriate permission and key before reuse. An Age value already represents time spent since origin generation or validation under the simplified model; local residence increases that age rather than restarting it. No-cache normally requires validation before reuse and differs from no-store, which prohibits the relevant storage. Track these decisions in order before using a body. The examples specify eligible requests and simple age accounting, excluding corrected-age network terms, authentication policy and invalidation except where named, so the exercise's assumptions do not silently override actual directives.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A response is Cache-Control:private, max-age=60. It is stored in the requesting user’s browser at age 0. The same eligible user request occurs 30 s later. Can that browser reuse it, and does private permit a shared proxy to store it?', 'Check freshness::age=30<60::The browser’s eligible response is still fresh.~Check cache scope::::Private permits the stated private browser storage rather than shared-cache storage.~Apply both decisions::::The browser can reuse its body; the shared proxy does not gain storage permission from its freshness.', 'Private browser reuse is allowed here; shared-proxy storage is not allowed by this directive.', 'Freshness and scope are independent:the same 60-second lifetime cannot override the explicit private restriction.'),
    E('application', 'A reusable response arrives with Age:40 and max-age=100. It then spends 50 s in a cache; ignore all other corrected-age terms. Find current age, remaining freshness and whether 20 more seconds preserves freshness.', 'Preserve prior age::::The response already carried 40 seconds of age when stored.~Add residence::age=40+50=90\\ s::Local storage does not reset origin age.~Compare the budget::::Ten seconds remain; after 20 further seconds age 110 exceeds max-age 100.', 'Current age 90 s; remaining 10 s; it becomes stale before 20 more seconds pass.', '100−90=10 agrees with expiry at sixty seconds after arrival because 40 seconds of the original budget were already consumed.'),
    E('trap', 'A stored response has Cache-Control:no-cache and a usable ETag. A student says this means “must never store it, ”so validation is impossible. Distinguish no-cache from no-store.', 'Read the reuse rule::::No-cache requires successful validation before ordinary reuse; it does not itself forbid keeping the representation.~Use the validator::::The cache can make a conditional request with the stored ETag and reuse the body if validation permits.~Contrast storage prohibition::::No-store is the directive that forbids the relevant storage, rather than merely requiring validation.', 'The claim is false; no-cache can allow storage with validation before reuse.', 'Keeping a body and validating it with a 304 demonstrates the no-cache workflow without treating it as no-store.'))

P('10-quantitative-percent', 'Quantitative aptitude', r'''
A quantitative word problem becomes easier when every unknown names a measurable quantity and every equation preserves units and reference bases. Fractions of an income add because they share one base, whereas mixture percentages must be weighted by their different volumes. Average travel speed uses total distance divided by total elapsed time; it is not automatically an arithmetic mean of the supplied speeds. Start with exact fractions, write the conservation relationship, and check the answer in the original wording. These exercises deliberately require budget conservation, mixture weighting and a counterexample about averages, rather than repeating one percentage multiplier with different numbers.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A person spends 3/5 of monthly income on rent and 1/4 on other costs, then saves 900. Both spending fractions refer to the original income. Find that income.', 'Add same-base spending::3/5+1/4=17/20::Both fractions can be combined because their base is the same monthly amount.~Find the remaining fraction::1-17/20=3/20::Savings are the unspent share.~Solve the amount::(3/20)I=900\\Rightarrow I=6000::Scale the known remainder to the full income.', 'Monthly income 6000.', 'Rent 3600 plus other costs 1500 plus savings 900 equals 6000, checking every given share.'),
    E('application', 'Mix 10 litres of a 20% solute solution with 5 litres of a 50% solution. Volumes add and no solute is lost. Find the final solute percentage.', 'Conserve each solute amount::10(0.20)=2,\\quad5(0.50)=2.5\\ litres::Percentages apply to their own volumes.~Combine conserved totals::solute=4.5,\\quad mixture=15\\ litres::Both amounts add under the stated assumptions.~Form the concentration::4.5/15=0.30::Divide solute by total volume rather than averaging the two percentages.', 'Final concentration 30%.', 'The 15-litre result contains 4.5 litres solute, exactly the two source amounts; 30% lies between 20% and 50%.'),
    E('trap', 'Travel 20 km at 30 km/h and then 20 km at 60 km/h, with no stops. Is average speed the arithmetic mean 45 km/h?', 'Compute each time::t_1=20/30=2/3\\ h,\\quad t_2=20/60=1/3\\ h::Equal distances do not imply equal travel times.~Use total quantities::D=40\\ km,\\quad T=1\\ h::Average speed is total distance divided by total time.~Evaluate the average::v=40/1=40\\ km/h::The slower leg occupies twice as much time and has greater time weight.', 'No; average speed is 40 km/h.', 'Forty kilometres at the calculated average takes one hour, the same sum of the two actual travel times.'))

P('10-quantitative-percent', 'Percentages', r'''
A percent change multiplies a specified base, and a subsequent change ordinarily uses the updated amount. Profit percentage usually refers to cost, while discount percentage usually refers to marked price; using the selling price for either can change the question. The difference between two percentage rates is measured in percentage points, whereas relative growth divides by the original rate. Keep these three denominators visible through the solution. Reversing a change requires dividing by its multiplier. The examples below distinguish a price discount and profit comparison, a rate change, and combined successive discounts, with exact decimal percentages and no rounding until final reporting.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'An item is marked 800, discounted 30%, and costs the seller 400. Find selling price and profit percent on cost.', 'Apply the marked-price discount::S=800(1-0.30)=560::Discount uses 800 as its base.~Find absolute profit::560-400=160::Subtract actual cost from the selling proceeds.~Choose the profit denominator::160/400=40\\%::Profit percent is relative to cost, not the marked or selling price.', 'Selling price 560; profit 40% on cost.', 'The 240 discount plus 560 price equals 800, and 400 cost plus 160 profit equals 560.'),
    E('application', 'An exam’s pass rate rises from 40% to 50%. Give its percentage-point increase and relative percentage increase in pass rate.', 'Compare the two rates::50-40=10::The direct difference is measured in percentage points.~Normalize to the original rate::(50-40)/40=1/4::Relative growth uses the old 40% rate as base.~Express both measures::::The relative fraction 1/4 is 25%, while the point difference remains 10.', 'Increase 10 percentage points and 25% relative to the original pass rate.', 'A 40% rate multiplied by 1.25 becomes 50%, confirming that relative increase and point change are different measures.'),
    E('trap', 'A shop applies 20% discount followed by 10% discount to an original price 100. A student adds them and predicts 70. Find the correct final price and net discount.', 'Apply the first reduction::100(0.8)=80::The first discount uses the original base.~Apply the second reduction::80(0.9)=72::Ten percent now means 8, not 10.~Compare with the original::100-72=28::The overall discount is 28% of the original 100.', 'Final price 72; net discount 28%, not 30%.', 'The combined multiplier 0.8×0.9=0.72 independently gives the same final amount.'))

P('10-quantitative-ratios', 'Ratios', r'''
A ratio describes relative quantities through one shared scale, and changing one part can change the ratio without changing every component. For a fixed total, divide by the sum of ratio coefficients; for a mixture change, retain the unchanged substance and introduce an unknown for the added substance. Weighted averages use the actual group sizes rather than merely the number of groups. Ratios are dimensionless only after compatible units are used, so a quantity called parts must still be translated into litres, people or money when required. The examples use additive amounts and exact values, checking both the new total and the required relative relationship after solving.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Divide 140 in ratio 5:2 between A and B. Find both shares.', 'Define one part::5k+2k=140::Both amounts use the same scale.~Solve the scale::k=140/7=20::Seven combined parts fill the total.~Recover the shares::A=5(20)=100,\\quad B=2(20)=40::Apply each coefficient to the common part.', 'A receives 100 and B receives 40.', '100+40=140 and 100/40=5/2, checking total and ratio independently.'),
    E('application', 'A 6-litre mixture has milk:water=2:1. Add only water until the ratio becomes 1:1. Volumes add. How much water is required?', 'Find starting components::milk=4,\\quad water=2\\ litres::The six litres contain three equal ratio parts.~Keep milk fixed::::Adding water does not change the four litres of milk.~Enforce the new relation::4=2+x\\Rightarrow x=2\\ litres::Equal milk and water require four litres of each.', 'Add 2 litres water; the final 8 litres contain 4 litres milk and 4 water.', 'The final ratio 4:4 is 1:1 and the original 6 litres plus the added 2 equals 8.'),
    E('trap', 'One class of 10 students averages 90; another of 30 averages 50. A student averages 90 and 50 to get 70 for all 40 students. Compute the correct combined average.', 'Convert averages to totals::10(90)=900,\\quad30(50)=1500::Group sizes determine each average’s weight.~Combine score and population::total=2400,\\quad count=40::Both groups must be represented in the denominator.~Divide the total::2400/40=60::The larger low-score group has three times the weight.', 'Combined average 60, not 70.', 'The weighted expression(1/4)90+(3/4)50 also gives 60; 70 would require equal group sizes.'))

P('10-quantitative-rates', 'Work rates', r'''
For independent constant-rate work on a divisible job, convert each completion time into jobs per unit time before combining contributions. When a participant joins later, the rate changes at that event and accumulated work from the earlier interval must be preserved. A drain or opposing activity contributes a negative rate; adding durations cannot express that cancellation. Check whether the remaining work can be completed before the next event, and whether the net rate is positive, zero or negative. The exercises assume uninterrupted constant performance, no interference and no capacity overflow effects, so each phase's rate times duration provides an exact conservation calculation.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A fill pipe completes a tank in 6 hours; a drain empties a full tank in 9 hours. Both run while an initially empty tank fills, with constant rates whenever water is present. Find fill time.', 'Convert signed rates::r=1/6-1/9::The drain removes part of the fill pipe’s contribution.~Combine fractions::r=1/18\\ tank/h::Positive net rate permits completion.~Invert the net rate::T=18\\ h::One tank requires eighteen hours at that rate.', '18 hours.', 'Over 18 hours the inlet supplies 3 tank-volumes and the drain removes 2, leaving one full tank.'),
    E('application', 'A completes a job alone in 10 days; B in 15 days. A works alone for 2 days, then B joins. Constant independent rates apply. Find total completion time.', 'Account for the first phase::W=2/10=1/5::A has completed one fifth before B arrives.~Combine later work rates::r=1/10+1/15=1/6\\ job/day::The remaining four fifths receive both contributions.~Finish and add phases::T=2+(4/5)/(1/6)=2+24/5=34/5\\ days::The second phase lasts 4.8 days.', 'Total 6.8 days.', 'A’s 6.8/10 contribution plus B’s 4.8/15 contribution equals 0.68+0.32=1 job.'),
    E('trap', 'A tank starts full. An inlet fills one tank in 10 hours and an outlet drains one in 5 hours. Both operate at constant rates until empty. A student adds the times and predicts 15 hours. Find the actual emptying time.', 'Assign opposing signs::r=1/10-1/5=-1/10\\ tank/h::Water decreases despite the inlet.~Track the initial volume::::One tank-volume is available to lose at magnitude 1/10 per hour.~Solve for zero::1-t/10=0\\Rightarrow t=10\\ h::Duration follows net removal rather than sum of solo times.', 'The tank empties after 10 hours.', 'The inlet adds 1 tank while the drain removes 2 during those 10 hours, resulting in the required net loss of one.'))

P('10-verbal-reasoning-passage-inference', 'Verbal aptitude', r'''
Verbal reasoning uses both grammatical structure and exact logical force. For agreement, identify the subject attached to the verb rather than the nearest unrelated noun. For inference, translate terms such as all, some and only if into their permitted directions before comparing options. Existence must be supplied before concluding that a universal category contains anyone, and a necessary condition does not become a sufficient condition merely because it is met. The exercises combine a grammar choice, a valid existential deduction and a countermodel for an unsupported converse. They rely only on the words given, without strengthening the passage using outside assumptions about selection or awards.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Choose the verb in “The list of required modules ___ on the desk”: is or are. Explain why “modules” does not control agreement.', 'Find the subject head::::The complete subject is the list of required modules, and its head is singular list.~Identify the modifier::::Of required modules describes the list; its plural noun is inside a prepositional phrase.~Agree with the subject::::The singular head takes is.', '“The list of required modules is on the desk.”', 'Removing the modifier gives“The list is on the desk, ”preserving the same agreement.'),
    E('application', 'Passage:All selected interns are certified. Some interns are selected. Does“Some interns are certified”necessarily follow?', 'Use the existence statement::::Some interns are selected supplies at least one actual selected intern.~Apply the universal rule::::Every selected intern, including that witness, is certified.~Retain the population::::That witness remains an intern, so at least one intern is certified.', 'Yes; some interns are certified necessarily follows.', 'The same supplied witness satisfies intern, selected and certified; no assumption that all interns were selected is needed.'),
    E('trap', 'Rule: A scholarship is awarded only if the score is at least 80. Ria scores 90. Does the rule alone guarantee Ria a scholarship?', 'Translate the direction::::Scholarship implies score≥80; the score is necessary for the award.~Inspect the supplied score::::Ria meets that necessary condition.~Test the converse::::A model where awards also require an interview and Ria receives none still satisfies the rule.', 'No; the rule alone does not guarantee the scholarship.', 'A high-scoring unawarded candidate violates no“only if”constraint, providing a counterexample to sufficiency.'))

P('10-verbal-reasoning-analytical', 'Analytical aptitude', r'''
Ordering constraints define a set of feasible arrangements, so answer words such as must and can quantify over that entire set. Treat an immediate-before pair as a fixed ordered block, then test which block positions leave room for all other restrictions. A fixed middle element can split available adjacent positions into separate cases. Prove uniqueness by eliminating alternatives rather than stopping after one solution. Conversely, a counterexample is enough to reject a must claim. These small examples use distinct occupants and one occupant per position, making exhaustive case checks practical while illustrating the same block-placement and quantifier discipline used for larger puzzles.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'A, B, C, D occupy positions 1…4. D is first, A is immediately beforeB, and C is afterB. Find the arrangement.', 'Fix the first occupant::::D takes position 1, leaving 2, 3, 4.~Place the ordered block::::A, B can occupy 2, 3 or 3, 4; the latter leaves no position afterB forC.~Complete the valid case::::PlaceA2, B3, C4, which satisfies both adjacency and after constraints.', 'D, A, B, C is the unique arrangement.', 'The only other block placement fails C’s required location; checking the final order confirms every original condition.'),
    E('application', 'Five positions hold A, B, C, D, E. E is third; A is immediately beforeB; C is beforeD. Enumerate all feasible arrangements.', 'Locate available block slots::::With E at 3, the adjacent A, B pair must occupy 1, 2 or 4, 5.~Fill the first case::::If AB occupies 1, 2, the remaining 4, 5 must beC, D, givingA, B, E, C, D.~Fill the second case::::If AB occupies 4, 5, the remaining 1, 2 must beC, D, givingC, D, E, A, B.', 'Exactly two arrangements:AB E CD and CD E AB.', 'Both respect E3 and the two ordered relations; there are no other adjacent pair slots that avoid E.'),
    E('trap', 'A, B, C fill three positions and the only constraint is A beforeB. A student concludes A must be first. Give a valid counterexample and explain the stronger wording error.', 'Preserve the relative order::::A beforeB permits another person beforeA.~Construct a witness::::C, A, B placesA second and B third.~Test the must statement::::The arrangement satisfies the only rule while A is not first, so the claimed universal position is false.', 'A need not be first; C, A, B is a counterexample.', 'Relative order A<B does not assign an absolute position; the witness checks both claims directly.'))

P('10-verbal-reasoning-spatial', 'Spatial aptitude', r'''
Spatial transformations become reproducible when points or marks are labeled. A clockwise quarter-turn maps Cartesian coordinates(x, y) to(y, −x), while a counterclockwise one maps to(−y, x); a reflection changes the coordinate perpendicular to its mirror axis. Compose operations in the supplied order because a reflection and rotation generally do not commute. For marked grids, compare the resulting arrangement with every allowed rotation rather than relying on an unmarked square's symmetry. The examples specify a fixed origin, ordinary Cartesian axes and distinctive labels, so orientation, order and handedness can be checked without assuming an unrestricted mirror is allowed.
''',
    E('fundamental', 'Rotate point(2, 1) clockwise 90° about the Cartesian origin. Use standard x-right, y-up axes.', 'Write the clockwise map::(x,y)\\mapsto(y,-x)::Both coordinates change together around the fixed origin.~Apply the point::(2,1)\\mapsto(1,-2)::The original positive x becomes a negative y coordinate.~Check distance::2^2+1^2=1^2+(-2)^2=5::Rotation preserves squared distance from the origin.', 'The rotated point is(1, −2).', 'Applying the inverse counterclockwise map gives(2, 1), confirming direction and reversibility.'),
    E('application', 'Starting at(1, 3), reflect across the x-axis and then rotate counterclockwise 90° about the origin. Compare reversing those operations.', 'Use the stated order::::Reflection gives(1, −3), then counterclockwise rotation gives(3, 1).~Reverse the order::::Counterclockwise rotation first gives(−3, 1), then reflection gives(−3, −1).~Compare compositions::::The two final points differ, so the operations are not interchangeable.', 'Stated order gives(3, 1); reversed order gives(−3, −1).', 'Both points retain squared distance 10, yet their different coordinates demonstrate that distance preservation alone cannot establish equality.'),
    E('trap', 'A 2×2 grid has rowsAB/CD. A vertical mirror producesBA/DC. Only rotations of the whole labeled grid are allowed. Can the mirrored arrangement be obtained by a rotation?', 'List quarter-turn outcomes::::The clockwise rotations giveAB/CD, CA/DB, DC/BA and BD/AC.~Compare the proposed grid::::BA/DC matches none of those four outcomes.~Use distinct labels::::Because every corner has a different mark, the complete enumeration distinguishes the mirror from all allowed rotations.', 'No; BA/DC is a mirror outcome unavailable by rotation alone.', 'Four successive quarter-turns exhaust the orientation states and restore the original grid, so no omitted rotation can supply the mirror.'))
